// X64Backend — the machine-code encoder. Turns a register-allocated,
// prologue/epilogue-inserted `TargetModule` into `.text` bytes plus the fixups a
// PE writer needs. Two public entry points:
//   emitFunctionChunk(module, func)  -> FunctionCodeChunk  (one function's bytes
//                                       with CHUNK-LOCAL fixup offsets)
//   concatX64FunctionChunks(...)     -> CodeResult         (lays out `.text` in
//                                       function order, rebases + resolves fixups,
//                                       and CLOSES `.text` with `__symtable`)
//
// THIN for maxon-shv2 M1: encodes exactly the TargetOps the `return 42` slice and
// its `mrt_start` entry stub produce — prologue/epilogue (`sub`/`add rsp, N`),
// ret, mov reg,imm (32- and 64-bit forms), 64- and 32-bit reg-reg moves,
// `call rel32`, and `call [rip]` through the IAT. Later milestones GROW the
// dispatch as the TargetOp union grows. arm64 is a separate backend at its own
// milestone.
//
// The encoders are also EXPORTED, because the panic runtime (X64Runtime) hand-assembles
// its functions from them rather than from TargetOps — see that file's header for why. A
// second encoder for an instruction this file already spells would be the project's
// signature bug (one fact written down twice) at the byte level, so the runtime gets these
// and mints none of its own. That is also why the exports are the ENCODERS and the OPCODE
// CONSTANTS, not a `encodeMov`/`encodeSub`/`encodeCmp` wrapper family: the caller naming
// the opcode is this file's existing convention (see `encodeRmRegDirect`).
//
// ENCODING FACTORING (pre-empting the duplication review): every instruction is
// assembled from shared primitives — `emitRex` (REX prefix with W/R/B bits),
// `emitRexRXB` (its SIB-index sibling that adds REX.X, for `lea [base + index]`),
// `emitModRmRegReg` / `emitModRmExt` (the mod=11 register-direct ModR/M byte),
// `emitModRmRipRel` (the mod=00 rm=101 `[rip + disp32]` ModR/M, shared by the IAT `call`
// and the runtime's address-taking `lea`),
// `encodeModRmDirect` (REX + optional 0F escape + opcode + ModR/M, the one place a
// register-direct instruction is assembled), its opcode-extension sibling
// `encodeModRmExtDirect` (REX + opcode + `/ext` ModR/M), its MEMORY-operand sibling
// `encodeModRmBaseDisp` (REX + optional escape + opcode + the `[base + disp]` operand — the
// one place a load, a 32-bit load, a store, a byte store, a `movzx` or a `lea [base+disp]`
// is assembled), the `[base + disp]` address assembler `emitBaseDispModRm` (ModR/M +
// optional SIB + disp8/disp32, the one place a base+displacement operand is encoded), and
// the `BinaryHelpers` little-endian writers.
// No REX/ModRM/SIB byte is hand-spelled per instruction: `mov`/`sub`/`cmp`/`and`/`or`/
// `xor`/`xchg` reg-reg all share `encodeRmRegDirect` (only the opcode differs), the
// two-byte `imul` and the 3-operand `imul r, r/m, imm32` reuse `encodeModRmDirect` (only
// the operand-to-field mapping is swapped — dest in reg, src in rm), and every
// OPCODE-EXTENSION form routes through `encodeModRmExtDirect` behind one encoder PER
// OPCODE GROUP rather than one per instruction — `encodeGrp1RegImm32` (cmp/and/or/xor
// `r/m, imm32`), `encodeGrp2RegImm8` + `encodeGrp2RegCl` (shl/sar by a constant / by CL),
// `encodeGrp3Reg` (neg/not/idiv), plus the `sub rsp`/`add rsp` frame pair. Both `lea`
// forms select their ModR/M mod field through `chooseDispMod` and emit their trailing
// displacement through `emitTrailingDisp`, so the rbp/r13 zero-disp8 SIB trap is spelled
// in exactly ONE place for both; both RIP-relative `lea` forms share `emitLeaRipRelPrefix`
// and differ only in which fixup table records their disp32.
// Signed immediates/displacements go through `writeImm32Signed` (one two's-complement
// converter). Opcodes, REX bits, and ModR/M/SIB constants are all NAMED below — no bare
// hex is scattered through the emitters.

// --- REX prefix (0x40 | W<<3 | R<<2 | X<<1 | B) ---
// REX.W selects 64-bit operand size; REX.R/B extend the ModR/M reg / rm fields to
// registers r8-r15. REX.X extends a SIB INDEX to r8-r15 — needed by `lea dest,
// [base + index]` (the 3-operand `+`).
let RexBase = 0x40
let RexW = 0x08
let RexR = 0x04
let RexX = 0x02
let RexB = 0x01

// Registers r8-r15 / xmm8-xmm15 (ENCODING number >= 8) need a REX.R/REX.B extension
// bit; their low 3 bits go in the ModR/M field. See `regNumber` for why the encoding
// number is the low NIBBLE of the rawValue and not the rawValue itself.
let ExtendedRegThreshold = 8

// The low nibble of an `X64Register.rawValue`: its ENCODING number within its own register
// file. Bit 4 is the FILE itself (0 = GPR, 1 = XMM) — see the enum's declaration, which
// places xmm0-15 at 16-31 precisely so this mask is all an encoder needs.
let RegisterEncodingMask = 0xF

// ModR/M mod field values (bits 6-7): 00 = no displacement (or, for a memory rm,
// disp0), 01 = disp8, 10 = disp32, 11 = register-direct (rm names a register).
let ModRmMod00 = 0x00
let ModRmMod01 = 0x40
let ModRmMod10 = 0x80
let ModRmRegDirect = 0xC0

// ModR/M rm field value 100: a SIB byte follows (for `[base + index]` and for a
// base register — rsp/r12 — whose low-3-bits are themselves 100).
let ModRmRmSibFollows = 4

// SIB byte fields: scale in bits 6-7 (00 = scale 1), index in bits 3-5, base in bits
// 0-2. Index field 100 with REX.X clear means "no index" (only rsp triggers this —
// see X64SibNoIndex); base field 101 with mod=00 means "no base, disp32" (so an
// rbp/r13 base is forced to mod=01) — see RbpR13Low3.
//
// ⚠ THERE IS NO `SibScale1` CONSTANT. The scale field's four values are `MemoryIndexScale`'s case
// list, and `memoryIndexScaleShift` is the one map to these bits — a second name for `byOne`'s shift
// would be the set written down twice, one of them unable to grow.
let SibScaleShift = 6
let SibNoIndexField = 4

// SIB base field 101 with mod=00 means "NO base register — a disp32-only absolute address" (the same bit
// pattern as rbp/r13's low-3, which is exactly why an rbp/r13 base is forced to mod=01 above). Used by
// `encodeMovGsAbsReg` for the segment-relative `gs:[disp32]` TIB writes.
let SibBaseDisp32Field = 5

// A base register whose low-3-bits are 101 (rbp / r13): with mod=00 the ModR/M or SIB
// base field 101 means "no base", so an rbp/r13 base MUST use mod=01 (disp8, zero when
// there is no real displacement). R13 is x64's silent-lea-miscompile trap.
let RbpR13Low3 = 5

// ⭐ **THE SIB INDEX OF AN x64 MEMORY OPERAND — this register, or `X64SibNoIndex` for none (EC16).**
//
// It is what lets `[base + disp]` and `[base + index*scale + disp]` be ONE address operand and
// therefore ONE assembler (`emitBaseDispModRmBits`). Every `[base + disp]` LOAD/STORE encoder in this
// file takes the pair `index` + `indexScale` with these defaults, so the 150-odd hand-assembly call
// sites that address `[base + disp]` say nothing about an index and the scaled-index band says exactly
// one thing more. (`encodeCmpRegBaseDisp` and the two GS-ABSOLUTE forms do not: nothing indexes a
// compare's memory operand, and a `gs:[disp32]` has no base register to index off.)
//
// ⚠ **THE "NONE" SPELLING IS x64'S OWN, NOT AN INVENTED SENTINEL.** SIB index field 100 with REX.X
// clear IS "no index register" — that is `SibNoIndexField`, the value `sibByte` is given for a
// bare `[base + disp]` — and 100 is rsp's encoding number. A register the ISA has already spent on
// meaning "absent" cannot also mean itself, which is exactly why rsp can never be a real index and why
// `x64SibIndex` PANICS on one rather than encoding it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS TWO SCALARS AND NOT A RECORD, AND THAT IS MEASURED.** A record-typed parameter with a
// `none()` default is CONSTRUCTED at every call, and these encoders run once per emitted instruction:
// the first cut of EC16 did exactly that and `scale-test` charged `phase:encode` **+47% allocations**
// (372,734 → 548,168 at rung 5) for it, against a control taken the same session. `StdArithOperands`'
// header records the same trap one tier up, for the same reason.
// ⛔⛔ **`public`, NOT `export`, AND THAT IS A HOLE IN E3092 RATHER THAN AN API-SURFACE CLAIM.** A
// DEFAULT ARGUMENT is evaluated in the CALLER's scope, so `X64GtRuntime` and `X64Runtime` — which call
// the exported encoders above without naming an index — resolve THIS NAME in their own files. MEASURED:
// drop the visibility and the build stops with 20 x `E2004 Undefined variable 'X64SibNoIndex'` at
// `X64GtRuntime.maxon:1089` and `X64Runtime.maxon:1098`, which is the compiler agreeing that the use is
// real and cross-file. `checkUnusedExports` cannot see it — it counts the names a file WRITES — so an
// `export` here is reported as E3092 and the SELF-COMPILE refuses the compiler's own source (it did, at
// `2cdfab05d5`). `public` is the one spelling that survives, and its documented meaning is exactly the
// statement needed: *"nobody here names it" is not a finding*. ⚠ The checker gap is real and is NOT
// fixed here — a default argument's names are cross-file uses E3092 structurally misses.
public let X64SibNoIndex = X64Register.rsp

// The colored register an INDEXED operand names, asserted to be a real index.
//
// A colored rsp reaching an index field would not be refused by the assembler — it IS `X64SibNoIndex`,
// so the operand would silently lose its index and address the wrong element. rsp is not allocatable,
// so a hit here is an allocator bug and not a user one. Every caller that has an index passes through
// this; the ones that have none pass `X64SibNoIndex` and never reach it.
function x64SibIndex(index X64Register) returns X64Register
	if index == X64SibNoIndex 'rspIndex'
		panic("x64SibIndex: RSP cannot be a SIB index — index field 100 with REX.X clear encodes 'no index' (`X64SibNoIndex`), so this operand would silently drop its index; the allocator must never colour an index to rsp, which is not allocatable")
	end 'rspIndex'
	return requireClass(index, class: RegClass.gpr)
end 'x64SibIndex'

// ModR/M rm field value 101 with mod=00: on x64 this combination does NOT mean `[rbp]`
// — it is repurposed as `[rip + disp32]`, the disp32 measured from the END of the
// instruction. Shared by `call [rip+disp32]` (through the IAT) and `lea r64, [rip+disp32]`
// (a runtime function taking the address of a label). It is the same rm slot as
// `RbpR13Low3`, and that collision is precisely WHY an rbp/r13 base cannot use mod=00.
let ModRmRmRipRelative = 5

// The ModR/M reg-field opcode extension of `call r/m64` (group-5 `/2`).
let Grp5ExtCall = 2

// The ModR/M reg-field opcode extension of `jmp r/m64` (group-5 `/4`) — the jump table's indirect branch.
let Grp5ExtJmp = 4

// --- Opcodes ---
export let OpcodeRet = 0xC3
export let OpcodeMovRmReg = 0x89  // mov r/m, r  (reg field = source) — the STORE form (spill)
let OpcodeMovRegRm = 0x8B  // mov r, r/m  (reg field = dest)   — the LOAD form (reload)
let OpcodeMovRm8Reg8 = 0x88  // mov r/m8, r8 (reg field = source) — the BYTE store form

// test r/m, r (85 /r) — a bitwise AND that sets flags and discards the result. `test reg, reg` sets ZF iff
// reg is zero, which is how the GT syscall shim checks `currentGt`/`stackBase` for null. EXPORTED because the
// shim (X64GtRuntime, hand-assembled) drives it through `encodeRmRegDirect` like the other reg-reg forms.
export let OpcodeTestRmReg = 0x85

// add r/m, r (01 /r; reg field = source), same shape as sub. EXPORTED because the hand-assembled
// `__gt_morestack` (X64GtRuntime) does several in-place `add`s (rebase rbp / a saved link by the relocation
// offset, compute new bounds) through `encodeRmRegDirect`.
export let OpcodeAddRmReg = 0x01
export let OpcodeSubRmReg = 0x29  // sub r/m, r  (reg field = source), same shape as mov
export let OpcodeCmpRmReg = 0x39  // cmp r/m, r  (reg field = source), same shape as sub

// cmp r, r/m (3B /r; reg field = DEST, r/m = source) — the LOAD-form compare, so `cmp reg, [base+disp]`
// computes `reg - [mem]` and sets flags in THAT direction (unlike `cmp [mem], reg` = 0x39, which negates them).
// EXPORTED because the GT stack guard (X64GtRuntime) needs `projectedRsp - [gt+stackGuard]` for its `jae`.
let OpcodeCmpRegRm = 0x3B
let OpcodeAndRmReg = 0x21  // and r/m, r  (reg field = source), same shape as sub
let OpcodeOrRmReg = 0x09  // or  r/m, r  (reg field = source), same shape as sub
export let OpcodeXorRmReg = 0x31  // xor r/m, r  (reg field = source), same shape as sub
let OpcodeXchgRmReg = 0x87  // xchg r/m, r (reg field = one operand), swaps r/m and r
let OpcodeMovRegImmBase = 0xB8  // mov reg, imm  (B8+rd; 32- or 64-bit by REX.W)
let OpcodeLea = 0x8D  // lea reg, [mem]  (reg field = dest, rm field = the address)
let OpcodeImulRegImm = 0x69  // imul r, r/m, imm32  (3-operand; reg = dest, rm = source)
let OpcodeGrp1Imm8 = 0x83  // group-1 ALU, r/m, imm8 (sign-extended)
let OpcodeGrp1Imm32 = 0x81  // group-1 ALU, r/m, imm32
let OpcodeGrp2Imm8 = 0xC1  // group-2 SHIFT, r/m, imm8 (a COUNT — never sign-extended)
let OpcodeGrp2Cl = 0xD3  // group-2 SHIFT, r/m, cl — the count is IMPLICIT, in CL
let OpcodeGrp3 = 0xF7  // group-3 unary ALU (TEST/NOT/NEG/MUL/IMUL/DIV/IDIV by /ext)
let OpcodeMovzxRegRm8 = 0xB6  // movzx r, r/m8 (two-byte 0F B6 /r; reg field = dest)
let OpcodeMovsxd = 0x63  // movsxd r64, r/m32 (REX.W 63 /r; reg field = dest) — the jump table's signed i32 load
let OpcodeCqo = 0x99  // cqo: sign-extend RAX into RDX:RAX (REX.W 99), no ModR/M
let OpcodeCallRel32 = 0xE8  // call rel32
let OpcodeCallIndirect = 0xFF  // group-5; with ModR/M /2 = call r/m64
let OpcodeJmpRel32 = 0xE9  // jmp rel32 (unconditional near jump)
let OpcodePushBase = 0x50  // push r64 (50+r) — 64-bit operand size by default in long mode
let OpcodePopBase = 0x58  // pop r64  (58+r) — the mirror of push

// The second byte of `syscall` (0F 05) — a two-byte opcode (the `0F` escape then this), with no
// ModR/M, no REX and no operands. The number goes in eax (loaded by the shared `encodeMovRegImm32`).
let OpcodeSyscallSecondByte = 0x05

// --- F2: atomics and the thread-identity read ---
//
// `LOCK` (F0) is an instruction PREFIX, not an opcode: it makes the read-modify-write memory operation
// that follows indivisible against every other core. Only a small set of instructions accept it, and all
// three atomics below are in it.
let LockPrefix = 0xF0
let OpcodeCmpxchgRmReg = 0xB1  // cmpxchg r/m64, r64 (0F B1 /r) — reg field = the SOURCE stored on a match
let OpcodeXaddRmReg = 0xC1  // xadd r/m64, r64 (0F C1 /r) — reg field = the addend IN and the prior value OUT

// The displacement every atomic's `[base]` operand carries. An atomic names an ADDRESS and nothing else —
// `StdOp.atomicCas`/`atomicRmw` have no field-offset operand, unlike `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` — so
// this is the only value it can take, and it is named rather than a bare 0 so that stays visible.
let AtomicWordDisp = 0 as Imm32

// The TEB self-pointer's offset within the Win64 TEB — `NtCurrentTeb()` reads `gs:[0x30]`. The pointer is
// established by the OS before a thread's first instruction and is never null, which is what makes it a
// legal answer to `StdOp.currentThreadToken`. Exported because `TargetPrinter` renders it in a golden.
export let WindowsTebSelfPointerOffset = 0x30

// --- SSE opcodes (all TWO-byte: the 0F escape, then this byte, under a mandatory prefix) ---
//
// The prefix is not optional decoration — see `SseMandatoryPrefix`. Each comment states the
// full encoding so the pair can be read as one fact.
let OpcodeMovsdRegRm = 0x10  // movsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 10 /r) — reg field = DEST  (the LOAD form)
let OpcodeMovsdRmReg = 0x11  // movsd xmm/m64, xmm (F2 0F 11 /r) — reg field = SOURCE (the STORE form)
let OpcodeAddsd = 0x58  // addsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 58 /r)
let OpcodeMulsd = 0x59  // mulsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 59 /r)
let OpcodeSubsd = 0x5C  // subsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 5C /r)
let OpcodeDivsd = 0x5E  // divsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 5E /r)

// ucomisd xmm, xmm/m64 (66 0F 2E /r). UNORDERED-signalling only for a SIGNALLING NaN, which
// is what makes it the right compare for IEEE's quiet predicates: it sets ZF=PF=CF=1 on a
// quiet NaN rather than raising. It writes ZF/PF/CF and leaves OF=SF=AF=0 — see X64CondCode.
let OpcodeUcomisd = 0x2E

// xorpd xmm, xmm/m128 (66 0F 57 /r) — a 128-bit bitwise XOR: the ONLY tool an XMM swap has (there is
// no `xchg xmm, xmm`, see `emitRegRegSwap`), and the last step of an f64 NEGATE (`encodeNegF64Reg`).
let OpcodeXorpd = 0x57

// pcmpeqd xmm, xmm/m128 (66 0F 76 /r) — compare dwords for equality. Against ITSELF it answers all
// ones in every lane, which is how an f64 negate builds its sign mask in a register with no `.rdata`
// constant to load (`encodeNegF64Reg`). SSE2, like the group-14 shifts below.
let OpcodePcmpeqd = 0x76

// cvtsi2sd xmm, r/m64 (F2 REX.W 0F 2A /r) — reg field = XMM dest, r/m = GPR source. REX.W is
// what selects the 64-bit source; without it this converts a 32-bit int.
let OpcodeCvtsi2sd = 0x2A

// cvttsd2si r64, xmm/m64 (F2 REX.W 0F 2C /r) — reg field = GPR dest, r/m = XMM source. The
// second `t` is TRUNCATE (toward zero), as opposed to `cvtsd2si`, which rounds by MXCSR's
// mode. Truncation is what `trunc()` means and what the spec's `3.14 -> 3` pins; the rounding
// form would answer 3 there and 4 for `3.99`, so the two are not interchangeable.
let OpcodeCvttsd2si = 0x2C

// sqrtsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 51 /r) — an ordinary two-byte scalar-double form, so it shares
// `encodeSseRegReg` with `addsd` and friends. NO REX.W: both operands are XMM, and the mandatory F2
// has already selected scalar-double.
let OpcodeSqrtsd = 0x51

// minsd / maxsd xmm, xmm/m64 (F2 0F 5D /r and F2 0F 5F /r) — two more ordinary two-byte
// scalar-double forms, so they too share `encodeSseRegReg`. NO REX.W: both operands are XMM and the
// mandatory F2 has already selected scalar-double. They compute `dest < src ? dest : src` and
// `dest > src ? dest : src`, which are the language's whole definitions of `min` and `max` — see
// `TargetOp.minMaxSdRegReg`. The two differ in this BYTE and in nothing else, which is why one op
// carrying a `FloatMinMaxKind` covers both.
let OpcodeMinsd = 0x5D
let OpcodeMaxsd = 0x5F

// The opcode byte a `minMaxSdRegReg` encodes to — the ONE place the target-neutral `FloatMinMaxKind`
// becomes an x86 opcode, the twin of `roundsdImm8` below.
function minMaxSdOpcode(kind FloatMinMaxKind) returns MachineWord
	return match kind 'k'
		min gives OpcodeMinsd
		max gives OpcodeMaxsd
	end 'k'
end 'minMaxSdOpcode'

// movq r/m64, xmm (66 REX.W 0F 7E /r) — reg field = XMM SOURCE, r/m = GPR DEST. The BIT
// REINTERPRETATION `float.hash` rests on: the same 64 bits, moved to the other register file, with
// no conversion and no rounding.
//
// ⚠⚠ ITS FIELD MAPPING IS THE `r/m, r` ONE — the reverse of `cvttsd2si`, which shares its direction
// (xmm → gpr) but writes its GPR dest in the REG field. Handing this opcode to `encodeSseRegReg`,
// which assumes `r, r/m`, assembles the two registers the wrong way round: legal bytes, a `movq`
// between the wrong pair, and NOTHING to report it. That is `encodeMovqGprXmm`'s whole content.
//
// ⚠ 66 is the mandatory prefix here (`packedDouble`), NOT the F2 of the scalar-double family: `F3 0F
// 7E` is `movq xmm, xmm/m64` — a different instruction, in the other direction. REX.W is required and
// is what makes it the 64-bit GPR transfer rather than `movd`'s 32-bit one.
let OpcodeMovqRmXmm = 0x7E

// movq xmm, r/m64 (66 REX.W 0F 6E /r) — the INVERSE reinterpretation, `bitcastI64ToF64`'s instruction:
// the same 64 bits moved back into the float file so a decimal reader's answer can be a `float`.
//
// ⚠ ITS FIELD MAPPING IS THE ORDINARY `r, r/m` ONE (reg = the XMM DEST, r/m = the GPR source), which is
// exactly what `encodeSseRegReg` assembles — so unlike its twin above this direction needs no encoder of
// its own. The asymmetry is Intel's: `0F 7E` is a STORE-shaped form and `0F 6E` a LOAD-shaped one.
//
// ⚠ REX.W is required for the mirror of `0F 7E`'s reason: without it `66 0F 6E` is `movd xmm, r/m32`,
// which would move 32 bits and leave the resulting double's exponent and sign zeroed.
let OpcodeMovqXmmRm = 0x6E

// --- The GROUP-14 SSE shift-by-immediate pair: f64 ABSOLUTE VALUE (`specs/abs.md`) and NEGATE ---
//
// `psllq xmm, imm8` is `66 0F 73 /6 ib` and `psrlq xmm, imm8` is `66 0F 73 /2 ib` — ONE opcode byte
// whose meaning is chosen by the ModR/M reg field, exactly like the group-1/2/3/5 GPR forms above, and
// the only SSE instructions in this file that carry an opcode extension instead of a second register.
// Each shifts BOTH 64-bit lanes; shv2 reads only the low one.
//
// ⭐ SHIFTING LEFT BY ONE AND BACK IS AN f64 `abs`: the sign bit falls off the top and zero fills in
// behind it, so bit 63 ends clear and bits 0-62 (exponent and mantissa) are exactly as they were. That
// is absolute value for EVERY pattern — `-0.0` becomes `+0.0`, `-inf` becomes `+inf`, and a NaN keeps
// its payload — because it is a bit operation and never an arithmetic one. Why shv2 clears the sign bit
// this way rather than with the `andpd` + `.rdata` mask BOTH reference compilers emit is recorded in
// full on `TargetOp.absF64RegReg`.
//
// ⚠ SSE2, unlike the SSE4.1 `roundsd` below — this pair does not move shv2's x64 baseline.
let OpcodeGrp14ShiftQImm8 = 0x73
let Grp14ExtPsrlq = 2
let Grp14ExtPsllq = 6

// The shift COUNT both halves of the sign-bit clear use: one bit, which is the sign bit's whole width.
let SignBitShiftCount = 1 as ShiftCount

// The sign bit's POSITION — what an all-ones lane is shifted left by to leave exactly bit 63 set, the
// mask an f64 negate XORs against (`encodeNegF64Reg`).
let SignBitPosition = 63 as ShiftCount

// --- `roundsd`: the ONE three-byte opcode in this file (SSE4.1) ---
//
// `roundsd xmm, xmm/m64, imm8` is `66 0F 3A 0B /r ib`. TWO things make it unlike every other SSE
// form here, and each is why it cannot ride `encodeSseRegReg`:
//   • its opcode is THREE bytes — the 0F escape, then a SECOND escape byte (0x3A), then 0x0B. The
//     shared `encodeModRmDirect` emits at most one escape (`twoByteEscape`), by design: bending it
//     into "0, 1 or 2 escapes plus an optional trailing immediate" would complicate the byte
//     assembler every GPR instruction in the file goes through, for one instruction.
//   • it carries a TRAILING IMM8 the shared assembler has no notion of.
// ⇒ It assembles its own prefix/escape bytes and then reuses the shared REX + ModR/M primitives, so
// the register-numbering and REX.R/REX.B rules are still spelled exactly once.
//
// ⚠ SSE4.1 — the first instruction in shv2's x64 output that is not SSE2. Every x86-64 part since
// ~2008 (Penryn / Bulldozer) has it; the baseline moves here and nowhere else.
let OpcodeThreeByteEscape3A = 0x3A
let OpcodeRoundsd = 0x0B

// `roundsd`'s imm8. Bits [1:0] are the rounding mode when bit 2 is clear; bit 3 is the PRECISION
// MASK, which suppresses the inexact (#P) exception.
//
// ⚠ BIT 3 IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET, and that is a divergence from `maxon-selfhosted` (which emits
// 0x08/0x09/0x0A). The VALUES are identical either way — the mask changes only whether an MXCSR
// flag is raised, and shv2 reads no MXCSR flag anywhere. The bootstrap, which is the oracle these
// results are matched against, emits the plain 0x00/0x01/0x02, so shv2 does too: a bit nobody reads
// is a fact written down for no reason.
let RoundsdImm8Nearest = 0x00  // round to nearest, TIES TO EVEN — `round(2.5)` is 2, not 3
let RoundsdImm8Down = 0x01  // toward -inf — `floor`
let RoundsdImm8Up = 0x02  // toward +inf — `ceil`

// Base of the two-byte `jcc rel32` opcode: after the 0x0F escape, `0x80 | cc` gives
// the conditional-jump opcode (e.g. `0x80 | 0x4` = 0x84 = JE). The `cc` nibble comes
// from X64CondCode's backing value.
let OpcodeJccRel32Base = 0x80

// Base of the two-byte `setcc r/m8` opcode: after the 0x0F escape, `0x90 | cc` gives
// the conditional-set opcode (e.g. `0x90 | 0x4` = 0x94 = SETE). The SAME `cc` nibble a
// `jcc` uses — one condition-code enum drives the branch and the materialization, so a
// fused compare and a materialized one can never disagree about the predicate.
let OpcodeSetccRm8Base = 0x90

// `setcc`'s ModR/M reg field is a fixed opcode extension of ZERO (`0F 90+cc /0`) — the
// instruction has one operand, and the reg field carries no register.
let SetccExtNone = 0

// Two-byte-opcode escape: a `0x0F` prefix byte introduces the two-byte opcode map
// (e.g. `imul r, r/m` is `0F AF /r`). `encodeModRmDirect` emits it when asked.
let TwoByteOpcodeEscape = 0x0F

// --- SSE mandatory prefixes ---
//
// An SSE instruction's IDENTITY is the pair (mandatory prefix, two-byte opcode): `0F 10` on
// its own is `movups`, under F2 it is `movsd`, under F3 `movss`, under 66 `movupd`. The
// prefix is part of the opcode, not a modifier of it — which is why it is spelled here beside
// the opcode bytes and named for the FAMILY it selects rather than for its hex.
//
// ⚠⚠ IT MUST PRECEDE THE REX BYTE, AND GETTING THAT BACKWARDS IS SILENT. x64 fixes the order
// legacy-prefixes → REX → opcode, and REX must be the LAST prefix before the opcode. Emit
// `REX F2 0F 58` instead of `F2 REX 0F 58` and the CPU reads the REX as a stray prefix that
// the following F2 supersedes — it decodes a different instruction, with no fault. That
// ordering is precisely why this rides the SHARED ModR/M assemblers as a PARAMETER (they emit
// it immediately before their own `emitRex` call) instead of being pushed by callers, who by
// construction could not get a byte in ahead of a REX the assembler has not written yet.
enum SseMandatoryPrefix
	// No mandatory prefix — every GPR instruction in this file.
	none
	// F2 — the SCALAR DOUBLE family: movsd, addsd, subsd, mulsd, divsd, and both converts.
	scalarDouble
	// 66 — every SSE form in this file whose mandatory prefix is NOT F2. This byte is ALSO the
	// operand-size override prefix, which is exactly why no generic path may be left to guess at it.
	//
	// ⚠ NO ROSTER, DELIBERATELY. This comment used to name its two members and went stale TWICE
	// without anything noticing — `roundsd` joined at P1.0d.4-C and `movq r/m64, xmm` at P1.7a's last
	// slice, and a reader who trusted the list would have concluded the 66 path had no encoder to
	// share. Each site states its own prefix at the call, which is the fact that cannot drift; grep
	// `packedDouble` for the current set rather than reading one here.
	packedDouble
end 'SseMandatoryPrefix'

let SsePrefixScalarDoubleByte = 0xF2
let SsePrefixPackedDoubleByte = 0x66

// The GS segment-override legacy prefix (0x65). On Win64 `gs:` addresses the Thread Information Block, whose
// StackBase/StackLimit the GT syscall shim repoints for a kernel call (see `encodeMovGsAbsReg`). Like the SSE
// mandatory prefixes it precedes the REX byte.
let GsSegmentPrefix = 0x65

// Emit an SSE instruction's mandatory prefix, if it has one. The shared ModR/M assemblers
// call this BEFORE `emitRex` — see `SseMandatoryPrefix` for why that order is load-bearing.
function emitSseMandatoryPrefix(code ByteArray, prefix SseMandatoryPrefix)
	match prefix 'p'
		none then break 'p'
		scalarDouble then code.push(SsePrefixScalarDoubleByte)
		packedDouble then code.push(SsePrefixPackedDoubleByte)
	end 'p'
end 'emitSseMandatoryPrefix'

// Second byte of the two-operand signed multiply `imul r, r/m` (0F AF /r). Unlike
// the `r/m, r` ALU forms, its ModR/M reg field is the DESTINATION — see
// encodeImulRegReg.
let OpcodeImul2ndByte = 0xAF

// Group-1 ALU opcode-extension (ModR/M reg field) values.
export let Grp1ExtAdd = 0  // ADD — exported: `__gt_morestack` (X64GtRuntime) does `add reg, imm32` (guard margin, +8 rsp fixup)
let Grp1ExtOr = 1  // OR  (the `or r/m, imm` immediate form)
export let Grp1ExtAnd = 4  // AND (the `and r/m, imm` immediate form)
export let Grp1ExtSub = 5  // SUB — exported: the GT syscall shim computes the TIB StackLimit as `sub rax, GtSystemStackSize`
let Grp1ExtXor = 6  // XOR (the `xor r/m, imm` immediate form — also the LOGICAL `not`)
export let Grp1ExtCmp = 7  // CMP (the `cmp r/m, imm` immediate form)

// Group-2 SHIFT opcode-extension (ModR/M reg field) values (C1 /ext ib, D3 /ext).
// `shr` (the Maxon keyword) lowers to SAR, not SHR: Maxon's `int` is SIGNED, so a
// logical shift would make `-8 shr 1` a huge positive number. The x86 mnemonic SHR
// (/5) is therefore deliberately absent — nothing in the language reaches it.
export let Grp2ExtShl = 4  // SHL r/m, count
let Grp2ExtSar = 7  // SAR r/m, count — ARITHMETIC right shift (sign-replicating)
export let Grp2ExtShr = 5  // SHR r/m, count — LOGICAL right shift (zero-filling)

// Group-3 ALU opcode-extension (ModR/M reg field) values (F7 /ext).
let Grp3ExtNot = 2  // NOT r/m — one's complement (writes NO flags, unlike NEG)
let Grp3ExtNeg = 3  // NEG r/m
let Grp3ExtImul = 5  // IMUL r/m — SIGNED 128-bit multiply RAX × r/m (low→RAX, HIGH→RDX)
let Grp3ExtDiv = 6  // DIV r/m — UNSIGNED divide RDX:RAX by r/m (quotient→RAX, remainder→RDX)
let Grp3ExtIdiv = 7  // IDIV r/m — signed divide RDX:RAX by r/m (quotient→RAX, remainder→RDX)

// 2^32 — used to fold a negative signed 32-bit immediate/displacement into its
// two's-complement low-32-bit encoding (see imm32Bits).
let TwoToThe32 = 4294967296

// ==========================================================================
// Shared REX / ModR/M primitives
// ==========================================================================

// The x64 ENCODING number of a register — what goes in a ModR/M reg / r/m field, a SIB
// field, or an opcode's +rd slot. rax=0..r15=15 AND xmm0=0..xmm15=15, because the two files
// have SEPARATE encoding spaces: `rbx` and `xmm3` are both register 3, and which one an
// instruction means is decided by its OPCODE, never by the number.
//
// ⭐ THAT is why this is a mask and not a lookup table, and it is the whole reason the one-enum
// design costs nothing here: `X64Register` places xmm0-15 at rawValue 16-31 so that the low
// nibble already IS the encoding number. Masking it at this one door makes every primitive
// built on it — `regLow3`, `regIsExtended`, and through them EVERY REX, ModR/M and SIB byte
// this file emits — serve both files with no XMM-specific path anywhere. v1 carries a parallel
// REX emitter (`emitXmmMemRexIfNeeded`, X64Backend.maxon:1930) for want of exactly this line,
// because it mints a second register enum whose numbers restart at 0.
//
// It is BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING for the GPRs: their rawValues are 0-15, where `x and 0xF == x`.
// The empty fragment diff across the whole suite is the proof, not the claim.
function regNumber(reg X64Register) returns MachineWord
	return (reg.rawValue as MachineWord) and RegisterEncodingMask
end 'regNumber'

// The register FILE `reg` lives in. `regClassOf` (RegBits) is keyed on the raw register
// NUMBER — the allocator's currency — so this is the dialect enum's one door to it, and the
// only place this file converts between the two spellings of a register.
function registerClass(reg X64Register) returns RegClass
	return regClassOf(reg.rawValue as RegNum)
end 'registerClass'

// The low 3 bits that go in a ModR/M reg or r/m field.
function regLow3(reg X64Register) returns MachineWord
	return regNumber(reg) mod ExtendedRegThreshold
end 'regLow3'

// True for r8-r15 (need a REX extension bit).
function regIsExtended(reg X64Register) returns bool
	return regNumber(reg) >= ExtendedRegThreshold
end 'regIsExtended'

// Emit the REX prefix for an instruction with a ModR/M reg field and r/m field.
// `wide` selects 64-bit operand size (REX.W). The prefix is emitted whenever it
// carries any bit: always for 64-bit ops, and for 32-bit ops only when a reg is
// extended (r8-r15). For opcode-extension forms (ModR/M /ext) the reg field holds
// an extension 0..7 that never needs REX.R, so callers pass a base register
// (`X64Register.rax`) as `regField`.
//
// `force` emits the prefix even when it carries NO bit (a bare 0x40) — required by
// exactly one instruction shv2 emits, `setcc r/m8`. In an 8-BIT register operand, the
// numbers 4..7 name AH/CH/DH/BH when NO REX is present and SPL/BPL/SIL/DIL when ANY
// REX is. rsi/rdi are allocatable argument registers here, so without the forced
// prefix `setcc sil` would silently assemble as `setcc dh` and set the wrong register.
// ⭐ **ONE REX EMITTER, ALL FOUR BITS.** `indexIsExtended` is the SIB INDEX's extension bit (REX.X) —
// the one field `regField`/`rmField` cannot express — and it defaults to `false`, which is the right
// answer for every operand shape that has no index at all (register-direct, `[base + disp]`, RIP-
// relative). It used to be a separate `emitRexRXB` differing in that one bit and in nothing else,
// which is the same fact written down twice, one bit apart.
function emitRex(code ByteArray, wide bool, regField X64Register, rmField X64Register, force bool = false, indexIsExtended bool = false)
	var rex = RexBase
	if wide 'w'
		rex = rex or RexW
	end 'w'
	if regIsExtended(regField) 'r'
		rex = rex or RexR
	end 'r'
	if indexIsExtended 'x'
		rex = rex or RexX
	end 'x'
	if regIsExtended(rmField) 'b'
		rex = rex or RexB
	end 'b'
	if wide or force or rex != RexBase 'needed'
		code.push(rex)
	end 'needed'
end 'emitRex'

// The two's-complement low 32 bits of a signed immediate/displacement, as an unsigned
// word `writeDword` can emit. A negative value wraps by adding 2^32 (the standard
// modular reduction), giving e.g. -8 -> 0xFFFFFFF8. `writeImm32Signed` is the caller.
function imm32Bits(value Imm32) returns MachineWord
	if value >= 0 'nonNeg'
		return value
	end 'nonNeg'
	return TwoToThe32 + value
end 'imm32Bits'

// Emit a signed 32-bit immediate / displacement field (little-endian two's complement).
function writeImm32Signed(code ByteArray, value Imm32)
	writeDword(code, value: imm32Bits(value))
end 'writeImm32Signed'

// ModR/M byte for a register-direct (mod=11) reg-to-r/m instruction.
function emitModRmRegReg(code ByteArray, regField X64Register, rmField X64Register)
	code.push(ModRmRegDirect or (regLow3(regField) shl 3) or regLow3(rmField))
end 'emitModRmRegReg'

// ModR/M byte for a register-direct (mod=11) instruction whose reg field carries
// a fixed opcode extension (e.g. group-1 /5 for SUB).
function emitModRmExt(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, rmField X64Register)
	code.push(ModRmRegDirect or (ext shl 3) or regLow3(rmField))
end 'emitModRmExt'

// Emit a mod=11 register-direct instruction from its parts: the REX prefix (with
// W/R/B), an optional two-byte-opcode escape (0x0F), the opcode byte, and the
// ModR/M byte. `regField`/`rmField` are the registers the ModR/M reg and r/m fields
// name — the caller maps DEST/SRC onto them, because the mapping is opcode-family-
// specific: the `r/m, r` forms (mov/add/sub) put SOURCE in reg and DEST in rm,
// while the `r, r/m` forms (imul) put DEST in reg and SOURCE in rm. This is the one
// primitive both `encodeRmRegDirect` and `encodeImulRegReg` build on, so the
// two-byte `imul` needs no hand-spelled REX/ModR/M of its own.
//
// `ssePrefix` is the SSE band's one addition, and it is emitted FIRST — before the REX, which
// is the only order x64 accepts (see `SseMandatoryPrefix`). It defaults to `none`, so every
// GPR caller is untouched and there is no second register-direct encoder for the SSE forms.
function encodeModRmDirect(code ByteArray, twoByteEscape bool, opcode MachineWord, regField X64Register, rmField X64Register, wide bool, ssePrefix SseMandatoryPrefix = SseMandatoryPrefix.none)
	emitSseMandatoryPrefix(code, prefix: ssePrefix)
	emitRex(code, wide: wide, regField: regField, rmField: rmField)
	if twoByteEscape 'esc'
		code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	end 'esc'
	code.push(opcode)
	emitModRmRegReg(code, regField: regField, rmField: rmField)
end 'encodeModRmDirect'

// Emit a mod=11 opcode-EXTENSION register-direct instruction's prefix + opcode +
// ModR/M: the REX prefix (always 64-bit here — every opcode-extension form shv2
// emits is 64-bit; the `/ext` reg field is an opcode extension 0..7 that never
// needs REX.R, so `regField` is the base `rax`), the opcode byte, and the ModR/M
// byte whose reg field carries the fixed extension. The opcode-extension analogue
// of `encodeModRmDirect` — the one place a `neg r/m` (`F7 /3`) or `sub/add rsp`
// (group-1) form is assembled; callers append any trailing immediate.
function encodeModRmExtDirect(code ByteArray, opcode MachineWord, ext MachineWord, rmField X64Register)
	emitRex(code, wide: true, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: rmField)
	code.push(opcode)
	emitModRmExt(code, ext: ext, rmField: rmField)
end 'encodeModRmExtDirect'

// Emit a `[base + disp]` MEMORY-operand instruction from its parts: the REX prefix (with
// W/R/B), an optional two-byte-opcode escape, the opcode byte, and the ModR/M (+ SIB +
// displacement) `emitBaseDispModRm` assembles. The memory-operand twin of
// `encodeModRmDirect`, and the ONE place a `[base + disp]` instruction is assembled — a
// 64-bit load, a 32-bit load, a store, a byte store and a `movzx` differ only in the
// arguments handed to it, so they cannot drift apart on the rsp-SIB / rbp-disp8 traps.
//
// `forceRex` exists for the 8-BIT register operand (see `emitRex`): with no REX prefix the
// register numbers 4..7 name AH/CH/DH/BH rather than SPL/BPL/SIL/DIL, so a byte store from
// rsi would silently assemble as a store from dh.
// `ssePrefix` mirrors `encodeModRmDirect`'s: emitted before the REX, defaulted to `none`, so
// `movsd xmm, [base+disp]` shares this assembler — and therefore the rsp-SIB and rbp-disp8
// traps — with every GPR load and store rather than forking a second memory-operand path (v1
// forks one: `emitXmmMemDisp`).
function encodeModRmBaseDisp(code ByteArray, twoByteEscape bool, opcode MachineWord, regField X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, wide bool, forceRex bool = false, ssePrefix SseMandatoryPrefix = SseMandatoryPrefix.none, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	emitSseMandatoryPrefix(code, prefix: ssePrefix)
	// REX.X extends the SIB INDEX. `X64SibNoIndex` is rsp, whose encoding number is 4, so the absent
	// case answers `false` through the very same test rather than needing a presence check of its own.
	emitRex(code, wide: wide, regField: regField, rmField: base, force: forceRex, indexIsExtended: regIsExtended(index))
	if twoByteEscape 'esc'
		code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	end 'esc'
	code.push(opcode)
	emitBaseDispModRm(code, regField: regField, base: base, disp: disp, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeModRmBaseDisp'

// ModR/M byte for a RIP-relative operand (mod=00, rm=101 — see ModRmRmRipRelative).
// `regFieldBits` is the raw reg field: a register's low 3 bits for `lea`, an opcode
// extension for `call /2`.
function emitModRmRipRel(code ByteArray, regFieldBits MachineWord)
	code.push(ModRmMod00 or (regFieldBits shl 3) or ModRmRmRipRelative)
end 'emitModRmRipRel'

// ==========================================================================
// Per-op encoders
// ==========================================================================

// Register-direct (mod=11) `opcode r/m, r` — the reg field is the SOURCE, the r/m field the
// DEST. That is the shared byte shape of EVERY single-byte reg-to-reg ALU form shv2 emits:
// `mov` (0x89), `sub` (0x29), `cmp` (0x39), `and` (0x21), `or` (0x09), `xor` (0x31) and
// `xchg` (0x87). All seven dispatch straight here from their `emitTargetOp` arm with only
// the opcode swapped — there is no per-instruction wrapper, because a function whose whole
// body is this call with a different constant is not an abstraction, it is a copy.
//
// `wide` selects REX.W (64-bit) vs 32-bit (which zero-extends into the full register), which
// is what also makes this serve the `mov ecx, r8d` ABI bridge. (`imul` does NOT route here —
// its operand-to-field mapping is swapped and its opcode is two-byte; see encodeImulRegReg.)
export function encodeRmRegDirect(code ByteArray, opcode MachineWord, dest X64Register, src X64Register, wide bool)
	encodeModRmDirect(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: opcode, regField: src, rmField: dest, wide: wide)
end 'encodeRmRegDirect'

// `imul dest, src` — the two-operand signed multiply `imul r64, r/m64` (0F AF /r),
// `dest = dest * src`, always 64-bit. Its ModR/M reg field is the DESTINATION and
// r/m is the SOURCE — the OPPOSITE mapping from the `r/m, r` forms above — and its
// opcode is two-byte, so it takes the escape path through the shared primitive
// rather than `encodeRmRegDirect`.
function encodeImulRegReg(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, src X64Register)
	encodeModRmDirect(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeImul2ndByte, regField: dest, rmField: src, wide: true)
end 'encodeImulRegReg'

// The GROUP-3 unary ALU forms (`F7 /ext`, always 64-bit, in-place on one register):
// `neg` (/3, `reg = -reg`), `not` (/2, `reg = ~reg`) and `idiv` (/7, signed divide
// RDX:RAX by `reg`). All three are byte-identical but for the extension nibble, so they
// share ONE encoder rather than three one-line wrappers around the same call. The /ext
// rides the ModR/M reg field, which is why this routes through `encodeModRmExtDirect`
// (with `sub/add rsp` and the group-1 immediate forms) and not the reg-to-reg path.
function encodeGrp3Reg(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, reg X64Register)
	encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeGrp3, ext: ext, rmField: reg)
end 'encodeGrp3Reg'

// The GROUP-1 register/IMMEDIATE forms (`REX.W 81 /ext id`, a SIGN-EXTENDED imm32):
// `cmp` (/7), `and` (/4), `or` (/1), `xor` (/6). Same prefix, same opcode, same ModR/M,
// same trailing immediate — only the extension differs, so the four share one encoder.
// The literal occupies no register in any of them.
export function encodeGrp1RegImm32(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, reg X64Register, imm Imm32)
	encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeGrp1Imm32, ext: ext, rmField: reg)
	writeImm32Signed(code, value: imm)
end 'encodeGrp1RegImm32'

// The GROUP-2 SHIFT forms, by a CONSTANT count (`REX.W C1 /ext ib`): `shl` (/4) and
// `sar` (/7). The trailing byte is a COUNT, not a signed immediate — it is never
// sign-extended, and `ShiftCount`'s 0..63 range is exactly the domain x64 distinguishes
// (it masks a 64-bit shift's count to 6 bits), so the byte is written verbatim.
export function encodeGrp2RegImm8(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, reg X64Register, count ShiftCount)
	encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeGrp2Imm8, ext: ext, rmField: reg)
	code.push(count)
end 'encodeGrp2RegImm8'

// The GROUP-2 SHIFT forms, by a VARIABLE count (`REX.W D3 /ext`): `shl` (/4) and `sar`
// (/7). The count is IMPLICIT — x64 reads it from CL and from nowhere else — so the
// instruction has no immediate and no second register operand. StdToX64Conversion is
// what puts the count in RCX (see `lowerShiftCl`).
export function encodeGrp2RegCl(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, reg X64Register)
	encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeGrp2Cl, ext: ext, rmField: reg)
end 'encodeGrp2RegCl'

// `setcc dest8` + `movzx dest, dest8` — MATERIALIZE a comparison's boolean into a full
// 64-bit register. Two instructions from one op, because the pair is indivisible:
// `setcc` writes only the LOW BYTE of its destination, so on its own it leaves the upper
// 56 bits holding whatever the register carried before, and the "bool" would compare
// unequal to both 0 and 1.
//
// The REX prefix is FORCED (see `emitRex`): rsi/rdi are allocatable here, and in an
// 8-bit operand WITHOUT a REX prefix, register number 6 is DH — not SIL.
function encodeSetccReg(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, cond X64CondCode)
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: dest, force: true)
	code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	code.push(OpcodeSetccRm8Base or (cond.rawValue as MachineWord))
	emitModRmExt(code, ext: SetccExtNone, rmField: dest)
	// `movzx dest64, dest8` (REX.W 0F B6 /r): reg field = dest (the 64-bit destination),
	// r/m field = dest (the 8-bit source setcc just wrote). REX.W is present, so the r/m
	// byte register uses the SPL/BPL/SIL/DIL encoding — no forced prefix needed here.
	encodeModRmDirect(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeMovzxRegRm8, regField: dest, rmField: dest, wide: true)
end 'encodeSetccReg'

// ==========================================================================
// The SSE band (P1.0d.4)
// ==========================================================================

// `<sse> dest, src` — the register-direct form of EVERY two-operand SSE instruction shv2
// emits. ONE encoder for all of them, because they differ in exactly two constants: the
// mandatory prefix and the opcode byte. That is the same rule `encodeRmRegDirect` states for
// the seven GPR `r/m, r` forms — a function whose body is this call with a different constant
// is a copy, not an abstraction.
//
// ⭐ It routes through the SHARED `encodeModRmDirect`, so the SSE forms inherit the REX and
// ModR/M bytes the GPR forms already use, and there is no parallel XMM encoder in this file.
// That is what the one-enum design buys, and `regNumber`'s low-nibble mask is what pays for it.
//
// The ModR/M reg field is the DESTINATION and r/m the SOURCE — the `r, r/m` mapping `imul`
// uses, NOT the `r/m, r` mapping of `mov`/`sub`/`cmp`. (`ucomisd lhs, rhs` reads rather than
// writes its "dest", but the FIELD mapping is identical, which is why it shares this encoder.)
//
// ⚠ `wide` is REX.W, and for SSE it is NOT an operand-size selector — the mandatory prefix has
// already chosen scalar-double. It is `true` for exactly the two CONVERTS, where REX.W selects
// the 64-bit GPR half, and `false` everywhere else; passing `true` for an `addsd` would emit a
// pointless REX and stop the bytes matching an assembler's.
function encodeSseRegReg(code ByteArray, prefix SseMandatoryPrefix, opcode MachineWord, dest X64Register, src X64Register, wide bool)
	encodeModRmDirect(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: opcode, regField: dest, rmField: src, wide: wide, ssePrefix: prefix)
end 'encodeSseRegReg'

// `movq dest, src` (66 REX.W 0F 7E /r) — `dest` a GPR, `src` an XMM, same 64 bits.
//
// It cannot ride `encodeSseRegReg` for ONE reason, and it is not `roundsd`'s: the byte sequence is an
// ordinary two-byte SSE form, but its ModR/M fields are the `r/m, r` STORE mapping (reg = the XMM
// SOURCE, r/m = the GPR DEST) where `encodeSseRegReg` hard-codes `r, r/m`. Passing the operands
// pre-swapped through that helper would put the inversion at the CALL SITE, where it reads as a bug
// and where a later editor would "fix" it; stating it once here, next to the opcode that owns it, is
// the same division `OpcodeMovsdRmReg`'s "reg field = SOURCE" comment already makes for the store form.
//
// Everything else is shared: the REX byte (REX.W required — without it this is `movd`, a 32-bit
// transfer that would silently drop a float's high half, i.e. its entire exponent and sign) and the
// ModR/M byte come from the same primitives every instruction in this file uses.
function encodeMovqGprXmm(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, src X64Register)
	encodeModRmDirect(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeMovqRmXmm, regField: src, rmField: dest, wide: true, ssePrefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble)
end 'encodeMovqGprXmm'

// `roundsd dest, src, imm8` (66 0F 3A 0B /r ib) — the ONE instruction here that `encodeSseRegReg`
// cannot assemble, for the two reasons `OpcodeRoundsd`'s comment gives in full: a THREE-byte opcode
// (a second escape byte the shared assembler emits no notion of) and a TRAILING imm8.
//
// It still spells nothing twice. The REX byte and the ModR/M byte come from the SAME primitives
// every other instruction in this file uses (`emitRex`, `emitModRmRegReg`), so the REX.R/REX.B
// register-extension rules and the reg/rm field mapping are stated exactly once for the whole
// backend; only the two bytes that are genuinely `roundsd`'s — its extra escape and its immediate —
// are written here.
//
// The ModR/M reg field is the DESTINATION and r/m the SOURCE (the `r, r/m` mapping, like every SSE
// form). ⚠ NO REX.W: both operands are XMM, and the mandatory 66 has already selected the form —
// REX.W would only lengthen the encoding and stop the bytes matching an assembler's.
function encodeRoundsdRegReg(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, src X64Register, mode FloatRoundMode)
	emitSseMandatoryPrefix(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble)
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: dest, rmField: src)
	code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	code.push(OpcodeThreeByteEscape3A)
	code.push(OpcodeRoundsd)
	emitModRmRegReg(code, regField: dest, rmField: src)
	code.push(roundsdImm8(mode))
end 'encodeRoundsdRegReg'

// `abs(x)` for an f64 already in `reg`: shift the sign bit out and shift the rest back
// (`OpcodeGrp14ShiftQImm8`'s comment carries the argument). IN PLACE, on one register — the op is
// two-address, so the allocator has already put the source there.
//
// It is the ONE encoder in this file that emits TWO instructions, and that is the shape the dialect
// deliberately chose: two Target ops would define one vreg twice, which the SSA the allocator runs on
// forbids. See `TargetOp.absF64RegReg`.
function encodeAbsF64Reg(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	encodeSseShiftQRegImm8(code, ext: Grp14ExtPsllq, reg: reg, count: SignBitShiftCount)
	encodeSseShiftQRegImm8(code, ext: Grp14ExtPsrlq, reg: reg, count: SignBitShiftCount)
end 'encodeAbsF64Reg'

// The ONE register `encodeNegF64Reg` builds its sign mask in. xmm5: caller-saved under BOTH ABIs shv2
// emits for (Win64 preserves xmm6–15, SysV nothing), so the prologue never saves it on this account, and
// the LAST float argument register, so it displaces the fewest live values. It is ALLOCATABLE, which is
// why `TargetOp.negF64RegReg` declares it on `implicitDefs` — the fill ladder's bargain, not the jump
// table's — and `assertEncoderScratchMasks` holds that literal equal to `x64NegF64ScratchMask()` below.
let NegF64MaskReg = X64Register.xmm5

// The negate's clobber, as the allocator's currency — the ONE statement of which register the mask
// build destroys (`TargetOp.negF64RegReg`'s inline literal is checked against it).
export function x64NegF64ScratchMask() returns RegMask
	return regMaskOf(NegF64MaskReg)
end 'x64NegF64ScratchMask'

// `-x` for an f64 already in `reg`: flip the sign bit. IN PLACE, on one register — the op is
// two-address, so the allocator has already put the source there — against a mask built in
// `NegF64MaskReg`: `pcmpeqd m, m` (all ones), `psllq m, 63` (bit 63 alone, both lanes), `xorpd reg, m`.
// Why a mask register rather than the `.rdata` constant both reference compilers use, or a shift trick
// like `abs`'s, is recorded in full on `TargetOp.negF64RegReg`. It is the second encoder in this file
// that emits more than one instruction, for the SSA reason `encodeAbsF64Reg` gives.
function encodeNegF64Reg(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	assertOperandOffEncoderScratch(reg, scratch: x64NegF64ScratchMask(), opName: "negF64RegReg")
	encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodePcmpeqd, dest: NegF64MaskReg, src: NegF64MaskReg, wide: false)
	encodeSseShiftQRegImm8(code, ext: Grp14ExtPsllq, reg: NegF64MaskReg, count: SignBitPosition)
	encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodeXorpd, dest: reg, src: NegF64MaskReg, wide: false)
end 'encodeNegF64Reg'

// ⭐ **ONE GUARD FOR EVERY ENCODER IN THIS FILE THAT OWNS FIXED REGISTERS** — the jump table's r10/r11 and
// the f64 negate's xmm5 — because they share one hazard: a colored operand that landed on a register the
// encoder clobbers would be destroyed before it is read. `TargetOp`'s `implicitDefs` forbids exactly this
// through `forbidOperandsFromImplicit`, and `assertEncoderScratchMasks` holds that literal equal to
// `scratch`, so a violation here is an ALLOCATOR bug, never a user program's. `opName` is carried because
// a union case cannot name itself, and the panic must send the reader to the right declaration.
function assertOperandOffEncoderScratch(reg X64Register, scratch RegMask, opName String)
	if regMaskContains(scratch, regNum: reg.rawValue as RegNum) 'aliasesScratch'
		panic("{opName}: an operand was colored to {registerName(reg)}, a register its encoder clobbers (scratch mask {scratch}) — `TargetOp.{opName}`'s implicitDefs must forbid its operands those registers (`forbidOperandsFromImplicit`)")
	end 'aliasesScratch'
end 'assertOperandOffEncoderScratch'

// One group-14 SSE shift-by-immediate (`66 0F 73 /ext ib`) — the opcode-extension form of an SSE
// instruction, which is why it can ride neither `encodeSseRegReg` (whose reg field is a REGISTER) nor
// `encodeModRmExtDirect` (which forces REX.W and emits no mandatory prefix, no escape and no immediate).
//
// It still spells nothing twice: the mandatory prefix, the REX byte and the ModR/M byte come from the
// same primitives every other instruction here uses, so the prefix ORDER and the REX.B rule for
// xmm8-15 are stated once for the whole backend. `regField` is the base `rax` for
// `encodeModRmExtDirect`'s reason — the `/ext` reg field is an opcode extension 0..7 and never needs
// REX.R — while REX.B still extends the r/m register. ⚠ NO REX.W: the mandatory 66 has already chosen
// the form, and REX.W would only lengthen the encoding and stop the bytes matching an assembler's.
function encodeSseShiftQRegImm8(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, reg X64Register, count ShiftCount)
	emitSseMandatoryPrefix(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble)
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: reg)
	code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	code.push(OpcodeGrp14ShiftQImm8)
	emitModRmExt(code, ext: ext, rmField: reg)
	code.push(count)
end 'encodeSseShiftQRegImm8'

// `roundsd`'s imm8 for a rounding mode — the ONE place the target-neutral `FloatRoundMode` becomes
// x86 bits, which is what lets the isel state the rounding once for both native targets.
function roundsdImm8(mode FloatRoundMode) returns MachineWord
	return match mode 'm'
		nearestTiesToEven gives RoundsdImm8Nearest
		towardNegative gives RoundsdImm8Down
		towardPositive gives RoundsdImm8Up
	end 'm'
end 'roundsdImm8'

// `movsd destXmm, [base + disp]` (F2 0F 10 /r) — the XMM twin of `encodeLoadRegBaseDisp`,
// through the SAME `[base + disp]` assembler, so the rsp-SIB and rbp-disp8 traps are spelled
// once for both register files rather than once per file (v1 spells them twice).
//
// ⚠ NO REX.W. The F2 prefix already selects scalar-double; REX.W adds nothing and would only
// lengthen the encoding. With `wide: false`, `emitRex` emits NO prefix at all unless a
// register is extended — which is what makes `movsd xmm0, [rax]` its correct 4 bytes.
//
// ⚠ THE MEMORY FORM ZEROES BITS 64-127 of the destination, where the register-to-register form
// MERGES them. Nothing in shv2 reads an XMM's upper half — every XMM value is a scalar f64 — so this
// is the right instruction both for a value SPILL (whose slot is 8 bytes because an f64 is) AND for
// preserving a CALLEE-SAVED xmm6-15 across a call, which Wave 2 does through the same 8-byte slot
// ops. Win64 nominally asks a callee to preserve all 128 bits, but that obligation is owed to
// EXTERNAL callers and shv2 has none; the full argument, and the condition that would invalidate it,
// is stated once in `X64PrologueEpilogue`'s header.
//
// ⚠⚠ BOTH OPERANDS ARE CLASS-GATED, AND THE REASON IS THIS PAIR'S CALLERS, NOT DEFENSIVENESS. They were
// EXPORTED at Wave 2 so the two hand-assembled GT chunks (X64GtRuntime's context switch and
// `__gt_morestack`) could preserve XMMs, and those hand off registers straight from an ordered LIST —
// `calleeSavedXmmOrder`, `argFpRegisters` — with no allocator and no class column between the list and the
// encoder. A GPR in either list assembles as the XMM sharing its low nibble (`rbx` → `xmm3`) and a GPR in
// the BASE position would address off the wrong register, both silently. That is exactly the doctrine
// `requireClass` states, applied to the two functions this commit widened the blast radius of.
export function encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp(code ByteArray, destReg X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeMovsdRegRm, regField: requireClass(destReg, class: RegClass.xmm), base: requireClass(base, class: RegClass.gpr), disp: disp, wide: false, ssePrefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp'

// `movsd [base + disp], srcXmm` (F2 0F 11 /r) — the store mirror of `encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp`, class
// gates included, for the reason stated there. The opcode is the ONLY other difference (0x11 puts the
// XMM in the reg field as the SOURCE, the same reg-field-is-source shape `mov r/m, r` has).
export function encodeMovsdBaseDispReg(code ByteArray, base X64Register, disp Imm32, srcReg X64Register, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeMovsdRmReg, regField: requireClass(srcReg, class: RegClass.xmm), base: requireClass(base, class: RegClass.gpr), disp: disp, wide: false, ssePrefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeMovsdBaseDispReg'

// `movsd dest, [rip + <.rdata constant>]` — materialize an f64 literal. The float twin of
// `encodeLeaRegGlobal`, and it files its fixup in the same `RdataRelocation` table with the
// same end-of-field convention; only the SECTION differs, which is what `RdataRelocKind`
// discriminates (`ripRelDisp32` is `.rdata`, `dataSectionRipRelDisp32` is `.data`).
//
// ⭐ WHY A CONSTANT POOL AND NOT AN IMMEDIATE: there is no `movsd xmm, imm64`. SSE has no
// immediate form at all — the same hardware fact that makes `foldConstOperands` refuse a float
// rhs and `lowerBinOpImm`'s `div` arm permanently unreachable. The IEEE bits ARE an integer we
// hold, but the only ways into an XMM are a load or a `movq` through a GPR, and the load costs
// no register.
function encodeMovsdRegRip(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, dest X64Register, label ByteArray)
	encodeModRmRipRelative(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeMovsdRegRm, regField: dest, wide: false, ssePrefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble)
	rdataRelocs.push(RdataRelocation.create(code.count(), rdataLabel: String.from(label), kind: RdataRelocKind.ripRelDisp32))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeMovsdRegRip'

// ==========================================================================
// Class-dispatched encoders — the class-agnostic ops
// ==========================================================================
//
// `movRegReg`, `storeSlotReg`, `loadRegSlot` and `xchgRegReg` are class-agnostic CONCEPTS: a
// move is a move whichever file it names (see TargetOp's SSE band for why the dialect has no
// float twin of any of them). The op therefore does not say which instruction it is, and these
// four helpers are where that is decided — from `regClassOf` of the RESOLVED register, which
// is the only place the answer exists.

// A register-to-register move: `mov` for a GPR pair, `movsd` for an XMM pair.
//
// A CROSS-CLASS pair panics rather than picking one. Moving an f64's bits into a GPR is `movq` —
// a different instruction with different semantics — and it is never asked for HERE: a value's
// class is fixed by its Std type at birth and phi copies join values of one type, so no MOVE ever
// spans the boundary. Every op that does span it — the two CONVERTS and the `movqGprXmm` BITCAST —
// carries its own TargetOp and its own encoder. So a mixed pair here is a coloring bug, and it is
// exactly the bug that would otherwise emit a `mov` naming a GPR that shares an XMM's low nibble.
function emitRegRegMove(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, src X64Register)
	let destClass = registerClass(dest)
	if destClass != registerClass(src) 'crossClass'
		panic("x64 emitter: a register-to-register move from {src.name} to {dest.name} crosses register files — a value's class is fixed by its type at birth, so the allocator colored a move's two ends into different files (every cross-class op — the two converts and the `movqGprXmm` bitcast — is its own TargetOp, so a MOVE is never one)")
	end 'crossClass'
	match destClass 'c'
		gpr then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: dest, src: src, wide: true)
		xmm then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeMovsdRegRm, dest: dest, src: src, wide: false)
	end 'c'
end 'emitRegRegMove'

// A 64-bit load from `[base + disp]`, dispatched on the DESTINATION's file. The one place a
// load picks its instruction — shared by the spill/reload path (`loadRegSlot`, base = rsp) and
// the data-section band (`loadRegBaseDisp` at word64, base = a global's address).
//
// ⭐ ONE encoder serves both BECAUSE A SPILL SLOT IS 8 BYTES AND AN f64 IS 8 BYTES. That is the
// whole reason floats leave the frame layout untouched, and the reason the splitter needs no
// float spill op: the slot the allocator already reserves fits the value it already has.
function emitWord64Load(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	match registerClass(dest) 'c'
		gpr then encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: dest, base: base, disp: disp, wide: true, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
		xmm then encodeMovsdRegBaseDisp(code, destReg: dest, base: base, disp: disp, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
	end 'c'
end 'emitWord64Load'

// A 64-bit store to `[base + disp]`, dispatched on the SOURCE's file — `emitWord64Load`'s
// mirror, shared by `storeSlotReg` and the data-section band's word64 store.
function emitWord64Store(code ByteArray, base X64Register, disp Imm32, src X64Register, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	match registerClass(src) 'c'
		gpr then encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: base, disp: disp, srcReg: src, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
		xmm then encodeMovsdBaseDispReg(code, base: base, disp: disp, srcReg: src, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
	end 'c'
end 'emitWord64Store'

// Swap two registers — SSA destruction's cycle-breaker, dispatched on their file.
//
// ⭐⭐ A GPR PAIR HAS `xchg`. AN XMM PAIR HAS NOTHING — there is no `xchg xmm, xmm`, at all — so
// it is swapped with the classic three-way XOR: `xorpd a,b; xorpd b,a; xorpd a,b` leaves a and
// b exchanged and touches no other register, no flag and no memory.
//
// ⭐⭐ WHY NOT A RESERVED SCRATCH + 3 MOVS, WHICH IS WHAT v1 DOES
// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Targets/Shared/CopyResolution.maxon:335-352`, `emitClassSwap` —
// whose CLASS-DISPATCHED SHAPE is exactly the one above; only the FP arm's substance differs).
// v1 reserves an xmm for its `fpCycleBreakOrd()`. The register it costs is not the reason to
// refuse it — FP pressure in Wave 1 is low, because every float across a call force-spills.
//
// THE REASON IS THE FAILURE MODE, and the two are not symmetric:
//   • This lowering's precondition is `a != b`, and it is established THREE LINES AWAY from the
//     only code that can violate it: `sequenceParallelCopy` calls `removeSatisfiedMoves` at the
//     top of every iteration, so the move it then breaks a cycle with cannot have `dst == src`.
//     It is asserted below regardless, AT the emit site, where it fails LOUD.
//   • A reserved scratch's precondition is "the allocator never colours a value into xmm15" —
//     which lives in the ALLOCATOR's pool mask, cannot be checked here (a swap looks identical
//     whether or not xmm15 holds something live), and fails as SILENT CORRUPTION.
// A precondition that can be asserted locally beats one that must be trusted across a file
// boundary, and this codebase's whole idiom is to prefer the loud failure.
//
// The performance argument for 3 movs does NOT survive contact, either: it rests on
// register-to-register `mov`s being renamed at zero latency, which is a GPR property. v1's own
// FP move is `emitMovXmmXmmOp(…, precision)` — a `movsd`, which MERGES bits 64-127 and is
// therefore NOT mov-eliminated and carries a false dependency on its destination. Both shapes
// are three serial-ish ops, on a path that exists only for a float phi-copy CYCLE.
//
// The garbage in bits 64-127 is not a problem and is worth stating: `movsd reg, reg` MERGES (it
// writes only bits 0-63), so an XMM's upper half holds whatever it last held. The XOR swaps all
// 128 bits, exchanging that garbage along with the value. Nothing reads it.
//
// `xorpd` rather than `xorps`: identical on the bits, but the packed-DOUBLE form keeps the
// register in the same domain as the `movsd`/`addsd` around it. A domain crossing costs a stall
// on some microarchitectures; it is never a correctness question.
//
// ⚠ THE SELF-SWAP GUARD IS NOT DEFENSIVE PADDING — IT IS THIS LOWERING'S ONE PRECONDITION.
// `a xor a` is ZERO, so a triple-XOR of a register with ITSELF does not swap it, it DESTROYS
// it — where the `xchg` this replaces would have been a harmless no-op, and where v1's 3-mov
// would too. `sequenceParallelCopy` cannot produce one (see above), so this can only fire if
// that loop's shape changes; it says so here rather than zeroing a live register at run time.
function emitRegRegSwap(code ByteArray, a X64Register, b X64Register)
	let aClass = registerClass(a)
	if aClass != registerClass(b) 'crossClass'
		panic("x64 emitter: a register swap between {a.name} and {b.name} crosses register files — a parallel-copy cycle joins values of one type, so this is a coloring bug")
	end 'crossClass'
	if a == b 'selfSwap'
		panic("x64 emitter: a register swap of {a.name} with itself — `sequenceParallelCopy` emits a swap only to break a cycle of length >= 2, and the XMM lowering's triple-XOR would ZERO the register rather than no-op on it")
	end 'selfSwap'
	match aClass 'c'
		gpr then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeXchgRmReg, dest: a, src: b, wide: true)
		xmm then encodeXmmSwap(code, a: a, b: b)
	end 'c'
end 'emitRegRegSwap'

// The three `xorpd`s that swap two XMMs — see `emitRegRegSwap` for why this is the lowering.
function encodeXmmSwap(code ByteArray, a X64Register, b X64Register)
	encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodeXorpd, dest: a, src: b, wide: false)
	encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodeXorpd, dest: b, src: a, wide: false)
	encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodeXorpd, dest: a, src: b, wide: false)
end 'encodeXmmSwap'

// `cqo` — sign-extend RAX into RDX:RAX (REX.W 99), so the following `idiv` divides the
// full 128-bit RDX:RAX. No ModR/M — both operands (RAX, RDX) are implicit — so it is
// just the REX.W prefix (emitted through the shared `emitRex`, which produces exactly
// 0x48 for a 64-bit op naming no extended register) followed by the one opcode byte.
function encodeCqo(code ByteArray)
	emitRex(code, wide: true, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: X64Register.rax)
	code.push(OpcodeCqo)
end 'encodeCqo'

// `lea dest, [base + index*scale]` — the 3-operand `+` at scale 1, and EC16's scaled-index address
// computation at 2/4/8 (REX.W 8D /r + SIB). No flags, and — because dest/base/index are independent —
// no copy. Both SIB traps belong to the SHARED assembler: an rsp index panics in `x64SibIndex`
// and an rbp/r13 base is forced to mod=01 by `chooseDispMod`.
//
// ⚠ ONE ENCODER FOR ALL FOUR SCALES, not a scale-1 encoder plus a scaled one: they differ in two bits
// of one byte, which `MemoryIndexScale` already names.
export function encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, base X64Register, index X64Register, scale MemoryIndexScale)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeLea, regField: dest, base: base, disp: 0, wide: true, index: x64SibIndex(index), indexScale: scale)
end 'encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale'

// `lea dest, [base + disp]` — `a + imm` / `a - imm` (REX.W 8D /r, signed disp). No
// index, so no REX.X; the shared `[base + disp]` assembler writes the REX, the ModR/M
// (+ SIB when the base is rsp/r12) and the displacement.
export function encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeLea, regField: dest, base: base, disp: disp, wide: true)
end 'encodeLeaRegRegImm32'

// ModR/M (+ SIB when the base is rsp/r12) + displacement for a `[base + disp]` memory
// operand. disp8 when it fits a signed byte, else disp32; a zero displacement still
// needs disp8 for an rbp/r13 base (mod=00 base=101 is RIP-relative, not [rbp]). One
// place the [base+disp] address form is assembled — no per-op respelling.
function emitBaseDispModRm(code ByteArray, regField X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	emitBaseDispModRmBits(code, regFieldBits: regLow3(regField), base: base, disp: disp, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'emitBaseDispModRm'

// The `[base + disp]` ModR/M (+ SIB + displacement) with the reg field given as RAW BITS — a register's
// low 3 bits for a memory load/store, or an OPCODE EXTENSION (`/2` for `call r/m64`) for a group-5 memory
// form. The one home of the rsp-SIB / rbp-disp8 traps, split from the register-taking `emitBaseDispModRm`
// exactly as `encodeModRmExtDirect` splits the extension form off `encodeModRmDirect`.
function emitBaseDispModRmBits(code ByteArray, regFieldBits MachineWord, base X64Register, disp Imm32, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	let baseBits = regLow3(base)
	let baseIsRbpR13 = baseBits == RbpR13Low3
	let modField = chooseDispMod(disp, baseIsRbpR13: baseIsRbpR13)

	// ⭐ AN INDEX FORCES rm=100 (a SIB follows) WHATEVER THE BASE IS — the SIB then carries the real
	// index — where a bare `[base + disp]` needs one only for an rsp/r12 base, whose own low-3 bits
	// ARE 100. Both spellings share `chooseDispMod` and `emitTrailingDisp`, so the rbp/r13 zero-disp8
	// trap is answered once for the whole family (mod=00 with base field 101 means "no base", so an
	// rbp/r13 base is forced to mod=01 with a zero disp8).
	if index != X64SibNoIndex 'indexed'
		code.push(modField or (regFieldBits shl 3) or ModRmRmSibFollows)
		code.push(sibByte(indexScale, indexBits: regLow3(index), baseBits: baseBits))
	end 'indexed' else 'noIndex'
		code.push(modField or (regFieldBits shl 3) or baseBits)
		if baseBits == ModRmRmSibFollows 'sib'
			// An rsp/r12 base has low-3 bits 100, which the rm field spends on "a SIB follows" — so the
			// real base has to move into the SIB, with the index field saying there is none.
			code.push(sibByte(MemoryIndexScale.byOne, indexBits: SibNoIndexField, baseBits: baseBits))
		end 'sib'
	end 'noIndex'

	emitTrailingDisp(code, modField: modField, disp: disp)
end 'emitBaseDispModRmBits'

// The trailing displacement byte(s) a `[base + disp]` ModR/M needs, keyed by the mod
// field `chooseDispMod` returned: nothing for mod=00, a signed disp8 for mod=01, a
// signed disp32 for mod=10 (the only three `chooseDispMod` returns). The one place the
// rbp/r13 zero-disp8 trap actually emits its byte — shared by BOTH `lea` forms (the
// `[base + disp]` assembler above and the `[base + index]` encoder).
function emitTrailingDisp(code ByteArray, modField MachineWord, disp Imm32)
	if modField == ModRmMod01 'disp8'
		code.push(imm32Bits(disp) and 0xFF)
	end 'disp8' else if modField == ModRmMod10 'disp32'
		writeImm32Signed(code, value: disp)
	end 'disp32'
end 'emitTrailingDisp'

// Pick the ModR/M mod field for a `[base + disp]` operand: mod=00 (no disp) only for a
// zero displacement on a non-rbp/r13 base; mod=01 (disp8) when the disp fits a signed
// byte; mod=10 (disp32) otherwise.
function chooseDispMod(disp Imm32, baseIsRbpR13 bool) returns MachineWord
	if disp == 0 and not baseIsRbpR13 'noDisp'
		return ModRmMod00
	end 'noDisp'
	if disp >= i8.min and disp <= i8.max 'disp8'
		return ModRmMod01
	end 'disp8'
	return ModRmMod10
end 'chooseDispMod'

// The SIB byte for `[base + disp]` with an rsp/r12 base (rm=100 forces a SIB): scale 1,
// index field 100 (no index), base in its field.
// THE SIB BYTE — scale in bits 6-7, index in 3-5, base in 0-2, and the ONE place those three fields
// are packed. Four call sites spelled it out before EC16: the no-index form an rsp/r12 base forces,
// the two GS-absolute `[disp32]` forms, and the scaled-index form. Only the last of them ever varies
// the scale, which is exactly why it is the one a future edit reaches for — and why the other three
// must not be a separate spelling of the same byte.
//
// ⚠ `scale` IS IGNORED BY THE HARDWARE WHEN THERE IS NO INDEX, so the three unindexed callers pass
// `byOne` — the identity, and the only honest answer for a field that scales nothing.
function sibByte(scale MemoryIndexScale, indexBits MachineWord, baseBits MachineWord) returns MachineWord
	return (memoryIndexScaleShift(scale) shl SibScaleShift) or (indexBits shl 3) or baseBits
end 'sibByte'

// `imul dest, src, imm32` — the 3-operand signed multiply (REX.W 69 /r id): reg field
// the DESTINATION, rm field the SOURCE, then a signed imm32. Reuses the shared
// register-direct assembly; only the opcode + trailing immediate differ.
function encodeImulRegRegImm32(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, src X64Register, imm Imm32)
	encodeModRmDirect(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeImulRegImm, regField: dest, rmField: src, wide: true)
	writeImm32Signed(code, value: imm)
end 'encodeImulRegRegImm32'

// `mov [base + disp], srcReg` — a 64-bit STORE (REX.W 89 /r, the `r/m, r` form: reg field
// = source, r/m = the memory operand). A spill names `rsp` here; the panic runtime names
// `rbp` (its frame slots) or a scratch register (a CONTEXT field). The base is a parameter
// rather than a hardcoded rsp precisely so those are ONE encoder: an rsp base forces a SIB
// byte and an rbp base forces a disp8, and both traps already live in the shared
// `[base + disp]` assembler this routes through.
export function encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code ByteArray, base X64Register, disp Imm32, srcReg X64Register, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, regField: srcReg, base: base, disp: disp, wide: true, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeStoreBaseDispReg'

// `mov destReg, [base + disp]` — a LOAD (8B /r, the `r, r/m` form: reg field = dest, r/m =
// the memory source), the mirror of `encodeStoreBaseDispReg`. `wide` selects REX.W (64-bit)
// vs 32-bit; the 32-bit form ZERO-EXTENDS into the full register, which is how the runtime
// reads the DWORD fields it must not sign-extend — an EXCEPTION_RECORD's ExceptionCode
// (0xC0000094 would otherwise become negative) and the symbol table's i32 columns.
export function encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(code ByteArray, destReg X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, wide bool, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeMovRegRm, regField: destReg, base: base, disp: disp, wide: wide, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeLoadRegBaseDisp'

// `cmp destReg, [base + disp]` — the LOAD-form 64-bit compare (3B /r, `reg, r/m`: reg field = the register,
// r/m = the memory operand), so it computes `destReg - [base+disp]` and sets flags in that direction. The GT
// stack guard (X64GtRuntime) uses it to compare a function's projected rsp against `[gt + stackGuard]` before
// a `jae`. Always wide (rsp/guard are 64-bit addresses).
export function encodeCmpRegBaseDisp(code ByteArray, destReg X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeCmpRegRm, regField: destReg, base: base, disp: disp, wide: true)
end 'encodeCmpRegBaseDisp'

// `mov [base + disp], srcReg8` — an 8-BIT store (88 /r), how the panic runtime's hex
// formatter writes one ASCII digit into its frame buffer. The REX prefix is FORCED for the
// AH/CH/DH/BH-vs-SPL/BPL/SIL/DIL trap `encodeModRmBaseDisp` documents.
export function encodeStoreBaseDispReg8(code ByteArray, base X64Register, disp Imm32, srcReg X64Register, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeMovRm8Reg8, regField: srcReg, base: base, disp: disp, wide: false, forceRex: true, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeStoreBaseDispReg8'

// `movzx destReg, byte [base + disp]` — load one byte, zero-extended to 64 bits (REX.W
// 0F B6 /r). The panic runtime's `strlen` reads a C string a byte at a time through this;
// zero-extension is what makes the `cmp dest, 0` NUL test correct for a high byte.
export function encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp(code ByteArray, destReg X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeMovzxRegRm8, regField: destReg, base: base, disp: disp, wide: true, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
end 'encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp'

// --- Intra-CHUNK rel32 resolution ---
//
// A function's blocks are laid out contiguously inside its ONE FunctionCodeChunk, so
// a `jmp`/`jcc` to another block never crosses a chunk boundary and is resolved here
// — not by `concat` (which only rebases inter-FUNCTION `call`/IAT fixups). This is
// the M4 analogue of `resolveCallFixups`, keyed by a label id within one chunk instead
// of by function name across chunks.
//
// Two DIFFERENT instruction shapes file fixups here, and they share this table because
// they share the one property that matters: an x64 rel32 / RIP-relative disp32 is always
// measured from the END of its 4-byte field, so the same patch arithmetic resolves both.
//   - `jmp`/`jcc rel32` to a block (`emitFunctionChunk`, keyed by `block.id`)
//   - `lea r64, [rip + disp32]` to a chunk-local label — how a hand-assembled runtime
//     function takes the address of a string it laid down at the end of its own chunk
//     (`X64Runtime`, keyed by ids it mints itself)
// Each fixup carries its own `PatchSite` so an out-of-reach displacement names the
// instruction that actually overflowed rather than a generic "block jump".

// One pending chunk-local rel32/disp32 site: the chunk-local offset of its 4-byte field
// and the label id it targets. `resolveChunkRel32Fixups` patches each once every label's
// offset is known (a forward jump targets a block emitted later in the chunk).
type ChunkRel32Fixup
	export var codeOffset as CodeOffset
	export var targetLabel as BlockId
	export var site as PatchSite

	export static function create(codeOffset CodeOffset, targetLabel BlockId, site PatchSite) returns ChunkRel32Fixup
		return Self{codeOffset: codeOffset, targetLabel: targetLabel, site: site}
	end 'create'
end 'ChunkRel32Fixup'

export typealias ChunkRel32FixupArray = Array with ChunkRel32Fixup

// `jcc rel32` / `jmp rel32` with a PLACEHOLDER displacement, each returning the chunk offset of its
// 4-byte field. The ONE spelling of each instruction's bytes, and the base of all four emitters below.
//
// ⚠ **THE PLACEHOLDER IS THE SHARED HALF, AND WHO RESOLVES IT IS THE DIFFERENCE.** A branch to a BLOCK
// records a `ChunkRel32Fixup` and is patched by `resolveChunkRel32Fixups` once the block layout is known;
// a branch INSIDE one macro-op's instruction sequence has no block to name — the Std→Target lowerings
// cannot create blocks at all (see `TargetOp.x64MemFill`) — and is patched by that encoder itself against
// a byte offset in this same buffer. Both go on to `patchChunkRel32`, so the "measured from the end of the
// 4-byte field" convention and the ±2 GiB guard are the shared ones rather than a second copy.
function encodeJccPlaceholder(code ByteArray, cond X64CondCode) returns CodeOffset
	code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	code.push(OpcodeJccRel32Base or (cond.rawValue as MachineWord))
	let dispOffset = code.count()
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
	return dispOffset
end 'encodeJccPlaceholder'

function encodeJmpPlaceholder(code ByteArray) returns CodeOffset
	code.push(OpcodeJmpRel32)
	let dispOffset = code.count()
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
	return dispOffset
end 'encodeJmpPlaceholder'

// `jmp rel32` to a block. The displacement is left zero and a fixup records its offset;
// `resolveChunkRel32Fixups` patches it once the target's chunk offset is known.
export function encodeJmp(code ByteArray, rel32Fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, targetBlockId BlockId)
	rel32Fixups.push(ChunkRel32Fixup.create(encodeJmpPlaceholder(code), targetLabel: targetBlockId, site: PatchSite.x64BlockJumpRel32))
end 'encodeJmp'

// `jcc rel32` to a block (0F 80+cc). `cond.rawValue` is the condition nibble; the displacement is a
// fixup like `encodeJmp`'s.
export function encodeJcc(code ByteArray, rel32Fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, cond X64CondCode, targetBlockId BlockId)
	rel32Fixups.push(ChunkRel32Fixup.create(encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: cond), targetLabel: targetBlockId, site: PatchSite.x64BlockJumpRel32))
end 'encodeJcc'

// `jcc rel32` BACKWARD to a byte offset ALREADY EMITTED into this same buffer — a branch whose target
// needs no fixup, because it is not a BLOCK: the stack probe's page walk and the fill ladder's store loop
// are each emitted as a unit, so the encoder is still holding its own loop label. `what` names the caller,
// so an out-of-reach displacement accuses the instruction that actually overflowed.
function encodeJccBackward(code ByteArray, cond X64CondCode, targetOffset CodeOffset, what PatchSite)
	patchChunkRel32(code, codeOffset: encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: cond), targetOffset: targetOffset, what: what)
end 'encodeJccBackward'

// Point a forward branch recorded by a placeholder emitter at the CURRENT end of `code` — the FORWARD twin
// of `encodeJccBackward`, and the other half of what a macro-op containing a size dispatch needs.
function patchLocalForwardBranch(code ByteArray, dispOffset CodeOffset, what PatchSite)
	patchChunkRel32(code, codeOffset: dispOffset, targetOffset: code.count(), what: what)
end 'patchLocalForwardBranch'

// --- The dense-match jump table (x64) ---

// The two FIXED scratch registers the jump-table sequence uses. r10 holds the table base then the target
// address; r11 holds the loaded i32 entry. Both are declared clobbered by `TargetOp.x64JumpTable`'s
// `implicitDefs`, which is what keeps the biased index off them (via `forbidOperandsFromImplicit`) and any
// cross-switch value off them (via the liveness clobber census) — see the op's header.
let JumpTableBaseReg = X64Register.r10
let JumpTableEntryReg = X64Register.r11

// `movsxd dest, [base + index*4]` (REX.W 63 /r + SIB scale=4) — the jump table's signed i32 slot load.
// The `lea` above with the `movsxd` opcode and a ×4 scale, through the SAME memory-operand assembler, so
// the rsp-index and rbp/r13-base traps are answered once for both. The base is always r10 here, so the
// base trap never fires; the general form keeps the encoder self-contained.
function encodeMovsxdRegBaseIndexScale4(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, base X64Register, index X64Register)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeMovsxd, regField: dest, base: base, disp: 0, wide: true, index: x64SibIndex(index), indexScale: MemoryIndexScale.byFour)
end 'encodeMovsxdRegBaseIndexScale4'

// `jmp r/m64` (FF /4, mod=11) — the jump table's register-indirect branch to the computed target address.
// The twin of `encodeCallReg` (FF /2) with the jump opcode extension.
function encodeJmpReg(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCallIndirect, ext: Grp5ExtJmp, rmField: reg)
end 'encodeJmpReg'

// ⭐ **THE JUMP-TABLE SEQUENCE.** `cmp idx, N` / `jae default` (UNSIGNED, so a wrapped negative index hits
// the default); `lea r10, [rip + table]`; `movsxd r11, [r10 + idx*4]`; `lea r10, [r10 + r11]` (the `add` as a
// flag-free `lea`); `jmp r10`. `N` (≤ 4096) fits the `cmp` imm32. The `jae` files a chunk-local rel32 fixup
// to the default block; the `lea` to the table files an `.rdata` reloc (patched to point at the blob); the
// per-slot block-address relocations are recorded in `jumpTables` and resolved after the block loop.
function encodeX64JumpTable(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, rel32Fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, jumpTables JumpTablePendingArray, indexReg X64Register, defaultTarget BlockId, caseTargets BlockIdArray, tableLabel ByteArray)
	assertOperandOffEncoderScratch(indexReg, scratch: x64JumpTableScratchMask(), opName: "x64JumpTable")
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: indexReg, imm: caseTargets.count() as Imm32)
	encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetBlockId: defaultTarget)
	encodeLeaRegRdata(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: JumpTableBaseReg, label: tableLabel)
	encodeMovsxdRegBaseIndexScale4(code, dest: JumpTableEntryReg, base: JumpTableBaseReg, index: indexReg)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(code, dest: JumpTableBaseReg, base: JumpTableBaseReg, index: JumpTableEntryReg, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeJmpReg(code, reg: JumpTableBaseReg)
	jumpTables.push(JumpTablePending.create(String.from(tableLabel), caseTargets: caseTargets))
end 'encodeX64JumpTable'

// The jump table's clobber, as the allocator's currency — the ONE statement of which registers the sequence
// destroys: `TargetOp.x64JumpTable`'s inline `implicitDefs` literal is checked against it by
// `assertEncoderScratchMasks`, and `encodeX64JumpTable` keeps its index operand off it — the encoder would
// clobber the index with the table base (r10) or the loaded entry (r11) before reading it.
export function x64JumpTableScratchMask() returns RegMask
	return regMaskOf(JumpTableBaseReg) or regMaskOf(JumpTableEntryReg)
end 'x64JumpTableScratchMask'

// --- F1b: the byte-fill SIZE LADDER (x64) ---

// The four FIXED registers `encodeX64MemFill` owns. The ISEL pre-moves the destination into the cursor and
// the byte count into the count register (`lowerMemFill`); the ladder then writes all four — it advances the
// cursor, decrements the count, pins the region's END so every arm can anchor a backward store on it, and
// holds the broadcast fill pattern.
//
// ⛔ **rdi AND rcx ARE THE TWO `rep stosq` DEMANDS, AND rax IS ITS IMPLICIT SOURCE** — those three are the
// instruction's, not a choice. rdx is the free one, and it takes the end.
//
// ⚠⚠ **ALL FOUR ARE ALLOCATABLE IN shv2, WHICH IS NOT TRUE OF THE v1 LADDER THIS PORTS.** v1's ABI makes
// rsi/rdi CALLEE-saved, so v1's memzero could pin rdi with nothing to declare; shv2's makes them ARGUMENT
// registers (`X64GtRuntime`: "the one place shv2's ABI diverges from v1's"), so a live value genuinely can be
// coloured into rdi and the clobber MUST be declared. It is — on `TargetOp.x64MemFill`'s `implicitDefs` —
// and `assertEncoderScratchMasks` holds that literal equal to `x64MemFillScratchMask()` below, so the two cannot
// drift apart the way only a comment would have kept them together.
export let MemFillCursorReg = X64Register.rdi
export let MemFillCountReg = X64Register.rcx
let MemFillEndReg = X64Register.rdx
let MemFillPatternReg = X64Register.rax

// The ladder's clobber, as the allocator's currency. `TargetOp.x64MemFill`'s inline `implicitDefs` literal is
// checked against this (`assertEncoderScratchMasks`), which is what makes the four `let`s above the ONE statement
// of which registers the fill destroys.
export function x64MemFillScratchMask() returns RegMask
	return regMaskOf(MemFillCursorReg) or regMaskOf(MemFillCountReg) or regMaskOf(MemFillEndReg) or regMaskOf(MemFillPatternReg)
end 'x64MemFillScratchMask'

// The ladder's band edges, in bytes. Each straight-line band is covered by `edge / 8` overlapping qword
// stores from EACH end, so the edge and the store count are one number rather than two that could disagree.
let MemFillQwordBytes = 8  // one qword store — the smallest width the overlap trick can anchor on
let MemFillTwoQwordBytes = 16
let MemFillFourQwordBytes = 32

// Bytes per iteration of the bulk store loop (8 qwords), and the lower edge of the loop band.
let MemFillLoopStride = 64

// Where `rep stosq` starts paying. Its ~20-40 cycles of startup dwarf the 1-8 plain stores a small
// size-class slot needs, and the dominant caller of this op zeroes exactly such a slot — so the ladder exists
// precisely to keep short fills away from it. (ERMSB/FSRM improve throughput and short `rep stosb`; they do
// not remove `rep stosq`'s setup.)
let MemFillRepThreshold = 256
let MemFillQwordShift = 3  // log2(MemFillQwordBytes) — bytes → qwords, for `rep stosq`'s count

let RepPrefix = 0xF3
let OpcodeStosq = 0xAB  // stos m64, RAX (REX.W AB) — store RAX at [RDI], advancing RDI

// `rep stosq` — store RAX into [RDI] and advance, RCX times. The one x64 instruction that IS a memory fill,
// and the ladder's arm from `MemFillRepThreshold` bytes up. DF is 0 by ABI on entry to and exit from every
// function and nothing in this backend ever sets it, so the store direction is forward.
function encodeRepStosq(code ByteArray)
	code.push(RepPrefix)
	// REX.W with no extended register — `emitRex`'s `wide` path emits exactly the 0x48 the instruction needs.
	emitRex(code, wide: true, regField: MemFillPatternReg, rmField: MemFillPatternReg)
	code.push(OpcodeStosq)
end 'encodeRepStosq'

// `count` consecutive qword stores of the fill pattern, from `[base + startDisp]` upward.
function encodeMemFillQwordRun(code ByteArray, base X64Register, startDisp Imm32, count MachineWord)
	for i in 0 upto count 'stores'
		// ⚠ The cast brackets the WHOLE sum — `a + b as Imm32` casts only `b`, `as` binding tighter than `+`.
		// Stated as its own `let` so the reading cannot be mistaken (the arm64 twin says the same).
		let disp = (startDisp + i * MemFillQwordBytes) as Imm32
		encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: base, disp: disp, srcReg: MemFillPatternReg)
	end 'stores'
end 'encodeMemFillQwordRun'

// ⭐ **THE OVERLAP TRICK, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES EVERY STRAIGHT-LINE BAND STRAIGHT-LINE.** For a length known to
// be at least `spanBytes` and less than twice it, write `spanBytes/8` qwords FORWARD from the cursor and
// `spanBytes/8` qwords BACKWARD from the end. Together they cover the whole region for ANY length in the
// band, with the middle written twice — so there is no loop and, crucially, no ragged-tail handling for a
// length that is not a multiple of 8. Zeroing a byte twice is free; branching to decide whether to is not.
function encodeMemFillOverlappingSpan(code ByteArray, spanBytes MachineWord)
	let qwords = spanBytes / MemFillQwordBytes
	encodeMemFillQwordRun(code, base: MemFillCursorReg, startDisp: 0, count: qwords)
	encodeMemFillQwordRun(code, base: MemFillEndReg, startDisp: -(spanBytes as Imm32), count: qwords)
end 'encodeMemFillOverlappingSpan'

// ⭐⭐ **THE FILL LADDER.** Fill `rcx` bytes at `rdi` with `fillByte`, choosing an instruction
// sequence by SIZE — which is the whole reason `StdOp.memFill` is an op rather than the per-byte Std-IR loop
// it replaced. The bands are ARCHITECTURE.md's ("The `memzero` the backend must emit"), and v1's
// `emitX64MemzeroOp` is where the shape was first debugged:
//
//   < 8       byte loop (rare — only a sub-qword fill reaches it)
//   8..63     straight-line OVERLAPPING stores, 1/2/4 qwords from each end
//   64..255   8-qword store loop, then one overlapping 64-byte block
//   >= 256    `rep stosq`, then one overlapping qword for the 0..7 ragged bytes
//
// `rdx` is pinned to the region's END up front, before anything mutates `rdi`/`rcx`, and left alone — that is
// what lets every arm anchor its backward stores without recomputing anything.
//
// A count of 0 falls into the `< 8` arm and exits on its first test, writing nothing.
function encodeX64MemFill(code ByteArray, fillByte ParsedInt)
	encodeMovRegImm64(code, dest: MemFillPatternReg, value: memFillPatternWord(fillByte))
	// end = cursor + count, computed BEFORE the ladder touches either.
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(code, dest: MemFillEndReg, base: MemFillCursorReg, index: MemFillCountReg, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)

	// The band dispatch. UNSIGNED throughout — a byte count is a length, never negative, and a signed
	// compare would send a >= 2^63 count (which no allocation can produce) down the wrong arm rather than
	// simply being unreachable.
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillQwordBytes)
	let toByteLoop = encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: X64CondCode.below)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillTwoQwordBytes)
	let toOneQword = encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: X64CondCode.below)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillFourQwordBytes)
	let toTwoQwords = encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: X64CondCode.below)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillLoopStride)
	let toFourQwords = encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: X64CondCode.below)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillRepThreshold)
	let toRep = encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual)

	// 64..255: whole 64-byte blocks, then ONE overlapping 64-byte block anchored at the end. The count is
	// at least 64 on entry, so `[end-64, end)` is always inside the region and needs no remainder test.
	let loopTop = code.count()
	encodeMemFillQwordRun(code, base: MemFillCursorReg, startDisp: 0, count: MemFillLoopStride / MemFillQwordBytes)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: MemFillCursorReg, imm: MemFillLoopStride)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillLoopStride)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: MemFillLoopStride)
	encodeJccBackward(code, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetOffset: loopTop, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	encodeMemFillQwordRun(code, base: MemFillEndReg, startDisp: -(MemFillLoopStride as Imm32), count: MemFillLoopStride / MemFillQwordBytes)
	let doneFromLoop = encodeJmpPlaceholder(code)

	// >= 256: `rep stosq` over the whole qword count, then one overlapping qword covering the 0..7 bytes the
	// qword count truncated away. The count is at least 256 here, so `[end-8, end)` is inside the region.
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: toRep, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	encodeGrp2RegImm8(code, ext: Grp2ExtShr, reg: MemFillCountReg, count: MemFillQwordShift)
	encodeRepStosq(code)
	encodeMemFillQwordRun(code, base: MemFillEndReg, startDisp: -(MemFillQwordBytes as Imm32), count: 1)
	let doneFromRep = encodeJmpPlaceholder(code)

	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: toFourQwords, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	encodeMemFillOverlappingSpan(code, spanBytes: MemFillFourQwordBytes)
	let doneFromFour = encodeJmpPlaceholder(code)

	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: toTwoQwords, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	encodeMemFillOverlappingSpan(code, spanBytes: MemFillTwoQwordBytes)
	let doneFromTwo = encodeJmpPlaceholder(code)

	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: toOneQword, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	encodeMemFillOverlappingSpan(code, spanBytes: MemFillQwordBytes)
	let doneFromOne = encodeJmpPlaceholder(code)

	// 0..7: a byte loop. The overlap trick needs a whole qword to anchor on and there is none here — and
	// this band is the rare one, reached only by a fill narrower than a word (a two-byte literal's buffer, a
	// single ranged element's vacated slot).
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: toByteLoop, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: 0)
	let doneFromEmpty = encodeJccPlaceholder(code, cond: X64CondCode.equal)
	let byteLoopTop = code.count()
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg8(code, base: MemFillCursorReg, disp: 0, srcReg: MemFillPatternReg)
	// The cursor advance FIRST: it writes flags, and the count decrement below is what the `jnz` reads.
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: MemFillCursorReg, imm: 1)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: MemFillCountReg, imm: 1)
	encodeJccBackward(code, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetOffset: byteLoopTop, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)

	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: doneFromLoop, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: doneFromRep, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: doneFromFour, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: doneFromTwo, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: doneFromOne, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
	patchLocalForwardBranch(code, dispOffset: doneFromEmpty, what: PatchSite.x64MemFillRel32)
end 'encodeX64MemFill'

// The bytes of `lea dest, [rip + disp32]` (REX.W 8D /r, mod=00 rm=101) UP TO — not
// including — the disp32 field. Split out because its two consumers file their fixup in
// different tables: a chunk-local label goes in the chunk's rel32 table, a named function
// in `callFixups` (which concat resolves by name across chunks). The bytes themselves are
// spelled once, here.
//
// REX.B must be CLEAR — in RIP-relative mode rm=101 names no register at all, so `rax` is
// passed as the rm field purely to keep the bit clear (the same convention the
// opcode-extension encoders use for their reg field).
function emitLeaRipRelPrefix(code ByteArray, dest X64Register)
	encodeModRmRipRelative(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeLea, regField: dest, wide: true)
end 'emitLeaRipRelPrefix'

// The bytes of a RIP-relative instruction UP TO — not including — its disp32 field: the
// mandatory prefix, the REX, the optional escape, the opcode, and the mod=00 rm=101 ModR/M.
// The THIRD addressing form's assembler, beside `encodeModRmDirect` (register-direct) and
// `encodeModRmBaseDisp` (`[base + disp]`), and the one place `[rip + disp32]` is spelled.
//
// Every caller leaves the disp32 to a fixup, because a RIP-relative displacement is never
// known at encode time — it names a target in another section or another chunk. What differs
// between callers is only WHICH TABLE the fixup goes in, which is why the disp32 is theirs to
// write and not this function's.
//
// REX.B must be CLEAR: in RIP-relative mode rm=101 names no register at all, so `rax` is
// passed as the rm field purely to keep the bit clear — the same convention the
// opcode-extension encoders use for their reg field.
function encodeModRmRipRelative(code ByteArray, twoByteEscape bool, opcode MachineWord, regField X64Register, wide bool, ssePrefix SseMandatoryPrefix = SseMandatoryPrefix.none)
	emitSseMandatoryPrefix(code, prefix: ssePrefix)
	emitRex(code, wide: wide, regField: regField, rmField: X64Register.rax)
	if twoByteEscape 'esc'
		code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	end 'esc'
	code.push(opcode)
	emitModRmRipRel(code, regFieldBits: regLow3(regField))
end 'encodeModRmRipRelative'

// `lea dest, [rip + <chunk-local label>]` — the address of a label in THIS chunk (a
// runtime function's inline string). Resolved by `resolveChunkRel32Fixups`.
export function encodeLeaRegLabel(code ByteArray, rel32Fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, dest X64Register, targetLabel BlockId, site PatchSite)
	emitLeaRipRelPrefix(code, dest: dest)
	rel32Fixups.push(ChunkRel32Fixup.create(code.count(), targetLabel: targetLabel, site: site))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeLeaRegLabel'

// `lea dest, [rip + <named .text chunk>]` — the runtime address of another chunk, e.g.
// `mrt_start` (the backtrace's `text_base`) or `__symtable`. It files an ordinary
// `CallFixup`: a `call rel32`'s displacement and a RIP-relative `lea`'s disp32 use the
// IDENTICAL end-of-field convention and both name a chunk, so concat's existing by-name
// resolution patches this with no new relocation kind.
export function encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, dest X64Register, targetName ByteArray)
	emitLeaRipRelPrefix(code, dest: dest)
	callFixups.push(CallFixup.create(code.count(), targetName: targetName))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeLeaRegFuncAddr'

// `lea dest, [rip + <.data global>]` — the address of a top-level `var`'s slot (P1.0d.5b).
// The THIRD consumer of `emitLeaRipRelPrefix`, and it files its fixup in a third table: an
// `RdataRelocation` of kind `dataSectionRipRelDisp32`, which the PE writer resolves against
// `.data`'s RVA once section layout is fixed.
//
// It cannot ride `encodeLeaRegFuncAddr`'s `CallFixup` path, and the reason is the section, not the
// instruction: a `CallFixup` names a `.text` CHUNK and concat resolves it by walking the function
// placements, whereas a global lives in `.data` and its offset comes from `GlobalDataTable`. Same
// bytes, same end-of-field convention, different table to look the target up in — which is exactly
// what `RdataRelocKind` discriminates.
export function encodeLeaRegGlobal(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, dest X64Register, label ByteArray)
	emitLeaRipRelPrefix(code, dest: dest)
	rdataRelocs.push(RdataRelocation.create(code.count(), rdataLabel: String.from(label), kind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionRipRelDisp32))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeLeaRegGlobal'

// `lea dest, [rip + <.rdata constant>]` — the address of a String literal's record (P1.2 wave A). The
// `.rdata` twin of `encodeLeaRegGlobal`: same instruction, same end-of-field convention, and it files
// the SAME `RdataRelocation` — only the kind differs (`ripRelDisp32` for `.rdata` vs
// `dataSectionRipRelDisp32` for `.data`), which is exactly what routes the PE writer to measure the
// disp32 from `.rdata`'s RVA rather than `.data`'s.
function encodeLeaRegRdata(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, dest X64Register, label ByteArray)
	emitLeaRipRelPrefix(code, dest: dest)
	rdataRelocs.push(RdataRelocation.create(code.count(), rdataLabel: String.from(label), kind: RdataRelocKind.ripRelDisp32))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeLeaRegRdata'

// Load `[base + disp]` at the op's width. Both arms REUSE an encoder the spill/reload path
// already had — `[base+disp]` addressing does not care whether the base is rsp or a global's
// address — which is the whole reason the review that unified the three drifted `[base+disp]`
// encoders into `encodeModRmBaseDisp` paid off here.
//
// The BYTE arm is `movzx`, not `mov`: a bool must arrive in the register ZERO-EXTENDED, or the
// upper 56 bits are whatever the register last held and `if flag` tests garbage. It is
// GPR-only — a one-byte type is never a float — so it asserts the file rather than dispatching
// on it.
//
// ⭐ THE WORD64 ARM IS WHERE AN f64 GLOBAL LOADS, AND IT NEEDED NO NEW WIDTH. `X64AccessWidth`
// stayed at two cases: the RESOLVED REGISTER'S CLASS already says whether those 64 bits move
// through `mov` or `movsd`, so a `float64` width would be that same fact written a second time,
// free to disagree with the first. `word64` is honest — an f64 IS 64 bits — and the shared
// `emitWord64Load` is the one place the instruction is chosen.
function emitDataLoad(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, base X64Register, disp Imm32, width X64AccessWidth, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	match width 'w'
		byte then encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp(code, destReg: requireClass(dest, class: RegClass.gpr), base: base, disp: disp, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
		word64 then emitWord64Load(code, dest: dest, base: base, disp: disp, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
	end 'w'
end 'emitDataLoad'

// Store `src` to `[base + disp]` at the op's width — `emitDataLoad`'s mirror, over the same
// byte and word64 encoders.
function emitDataStore(code ByteArray, base X64Register, disp Imm32, src X64Register, width X64AccessWidth, index X64Register = X64SibNoIndex, indexScale MemoryIndexScale = MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	match width 'w'
		byte then encodeStoreBaseDispReg8(code, base: base, disp: disp, srcReg: requireClass(src, class: RegClass.gpr), index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
		word64 then emitWord64Store(code, base: base, disp: disp, src: src, index: index, indexScale: indexScale)
	end 'w'
end 'emitDataStore'

// Patch a chunk-local rel32 field to the chunk-local start offset of its target.
// The displacement is measured from the END of the 4-byte field (`codeOffset +
// X64Disp32Width`) — the shared x64 `call`/`jmp`/`jcc` rel32 convention — so this
// one helper backs BOTH the inter-function call fixups (resolveCallFixups) and the
// intra-chunk rel32 fixups (resolveChunkRel32Fixups) rather than respelling the
// displacement math at each. `patchX64Rel32` guards the ±2 GiB reach.
function patchChunkRel32(code ByteArray, codeOffset CodeOffset, targetOffset CodeOffset, what PatchSite)
	let rel = targetOffset - (codeOffset + X64Disp32Width)
	patchX64Rel32(code, codeOffset: codeOffset, rel: rel, what: what, targetAddr: targetOffset)
end 'patchChunkRel32'

// Patch every chunk-local rel32/disp32 to the chunk-local displacement of its target label
// (a forward jump targets a block emitted later in the chunk; a runtime string label is
// always emitted after the code that references it).
export function resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code ByteArray, labelOffsets BlockOffsetTable, rel32Fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray)
	for fixup in rel32Fixups 'resolve'
		let targetOffset = try labelOffsets.get(fixup.targetLabel) otherwise panic("resolveChunkRel32Fixups: {fixup.site.name} at chunk offset {fixup.codeOffset} targets label {fixup.targetLabel}, which was never laid out in this chunk")
		patchChunkRel32(code, codeOffset: fixup.codeOffset, targetOffset: targetOffset, what: fixup.site)
	end 'resolve'
end 'resolveChunkRel32Fixups'

// mov reg32, imm32 (B8+rd id). Writing a 32-bit register zero-extends to 64 bits,
// so this is the compact form for any value that fits u32 (e.g. the literal 42).
export function encodeMovRegImm32(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, value MachineWord)
	// REX.B only when `dest` is r8-r15 (the opcode's +rd register — there is no
	// ModR/M byte). The shared emitRex applies the "emit only when a bit is set"
	// rule, so no reg needs REX for rax-rdi; a base register in `regField` keeps
	// REX.R clear (mirrors encodeMovRegImm64, which differs only in REX.W).
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: dest)
	code.push(OpcodeMovRegImmBase + regLow3(dest))
	writeDword(code, value: value)
end 'encodeMovRegImm32'

// mov reg64, imm64 (REX.W B8+rd io). Reached for a value OUTSIDE the unsigned 32-bit
// domain — either wider than u32.max or NEGATIVE (the `constMaterializationOp` size
// choice routes only 0..u32.max to the zero-extending 32-bit form).
export function encodeMovRegImm64(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, value MachineWord)
	emitRex(code, wide: true, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: dest)
	code.push(OpcodeMovRegImmBase + regLow3(dest))
	writeQword(code, value: value)
end 'encodeMovRegImm64'

// sub/add rsp, imm — the rsp-only frame adjustment. `ext` picks SUB (prologue) vs
// ADD (epilogue); the two differ only in the ModR/M opcode-extension. imm8 form
// when it fits a signed byte, else imm32. Shares the opcode-extension assembly
// (`encodeModRmExtDirect`) with `neg`; only the trailing immediate width differs.
//
// ⚠ THE imm32 IS SIGN-EXTENDED TO 64 BITS, so the widest frame this form can express is
// `i32.max` — not `u32.max`. Writing the amount through `writeImm32Signed` makes a frame past
// that a LOUD range panic on the cast; a raw `writeDword` would emit a NEGATIVE immediate and
// `sub rsp, <negative>` moves rsp UP, handing the function a frame that overlaps its caller's.
function encodeRspAdjust(code ByteArray, ext MachineWord, amount StackSize)
	if amount <= i8.max 'imm8'
		encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeGrp1Imm8, ext: ext, rmField: X64Register.rsp)
		code.push(amount and 0xFF)
	end 'imm8' else 'imm32'
		encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeGrp1Imm32, ext: ext, rmField: X64Register.rsp)
		writeImm32Signed(code, value: amount as Imm32)
	end 'imm32'
end 'encodeRspAdjust'

// push r64 (50+r). 64-bit operand size is the default in long mode, so NO REX.W —
// only REX.B when the register is r8–r15 (r12–r15 are the callee-saved regs the
// prologue pushes). `emitRex` with `wide: false` emits exactly that: nothing for
// rax–rdi/rbx, `0x41` for an extended register.
//
// ⚠⚠ GPR-ONLY, AND THE CHECK IS NOT DEFENSIVE PADDING — **THERE IS NO `push xmm` IN x86-64.** This
// encoder takes a register's low 3 bits and emits `push r64` unconditionally, so an XMM handed to it
// assembles as the GPR sharing its low nibble: xmm3 and rbx both encode as 3, and the result is a
// push of a register nobody named, with no error at any layer. That is precisely the mistake Wave 2
// invited — the obvious way to make xmm6–15 callee-saved is to add them to `calleeSavedOrder`, which
// this loop iterates. `requireClass` turns "there is no push xmm" from something a reader has to
// remember into something the compiler states. (What Wave 2 does instead: frame slots, see
// X64PrologueEpilogue.)
export function encodePushReg(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	let gpr = requireClass(reg, class: RegClass.gpr)
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: gpr)
	code.push(OpcodePushBase + regLow3(gpr))
end 'encodePushReg'

// pop r64 (58+r) — the exact mirror of `encodePushReg`, class gate included.
export function encodePopReg(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	let gpr = requireClass(reg, class: RegClass.gpr)
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: gpr)
	code.push(OpcodePopBase + regLow3(gpr))
end 'encodePopReg'

// The bytes a stack grows by when its GUARD PAGE is touched, and therefore the largest frame a
// single `sub rsp` may open. Windows commits a thread stack ONE PAGE AT A TIME, and only when the
// guard page itself is accessed: a subtraction that carries rsp PAST the guard page never touches
// it, so the first write below lands on reserved-but-uncommitted memory and the process dies with
// 0xC0000005. A frame of exactly one page cannot skip it — the deepest address it reaches is
// `rsp - 4096`, and the guard page starts no lower than that whatever rsp's offset within its own
// page — so the walk is needed strictly PAST this size, which is why the test below is `>`.
//
// It is the x86-64 page size, an ARCHITECTURE fact about the address translation the OS grows
// stacks in units of. The PE writer's section alignment happens to be the same number for an
// entirely unrelated reason; tying the two together would be one constant standing for two facts.
let StackGuardPageBytes = 4096 as StackSize

// The register the page walk borrows. It must hold no live value AT THE PROLOGUE, which rules out
// every argument register: the prologue runs before a single incoming argument has been captured.
// r11 carries no argument in EITHER ABI shv2 emits for (Win64 rcx/rdx/r8/r9, SysV
// rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9), it is caller-saved in both, and `emitGtStackGuard` — emitted immediately
// BEFORE this, at the same function entry — already clobbers it, so nothing live can be sitting
// there. It is the same register the bootstrap's `__chkstk` takes its size in, and for the same
// reason: that helper originally used RAX and silently destroyed parameter 7 of every function
// with a frame past a page (`specs-shv2/x64-large-frame-arg7.md`, which is this file's live guard
// on the choice).
let StackProbeScratchReg = X64Register.r11

// prologue: reserve the frame. A zero-size frame emits nothing (a frameless function never carries
// a prologue op, but the guard keeps the encoder total); a frame within one page is a bare
// `sub rsp, stackSize`; a LARGER one must walk the pages down (see `encodeStackProbe`).
//
// `probeScratchIsFree` is the caller's certificate that `StackProbeScratchReg` holds nothing live
// here. Only a FUNCTION ENTRY can say yes, and only a function entry has an unbounded frame — the
// hand-assembled runtime chunks call this MID-STREAM with a fixed, page-sized-at-most reservation
// while r11 carries a live value (the GT trampoline's function pointer, the syscall shim's
// currentGt, `__gt_morestack`'s gt). The threshold is what keeps those safe, and the certificate
// is what turns "they are all small" from a comment that ages into a panic that fires.
export function encodePrologue(code ByteArray, stackSize StackSize, probeScratchIsFree bool)
	if stackSize > StackGuardPageBytes 'walk'
		if not probeScratchIsFree 'noScratch'
			// Bound to a local because the bootstrap's parser rejects a member access on a
			// module-level `let` inside an interpolation (`{StackProbeScratchReg.name}` is an
			// E2010 while `{scratch.name}` parses).
			let scratch = StackProbeScratchReg
			panic("encodePrologue: a {stackSize}-byte frame is past the {StackGuardPageBytes}-byte guard page, so it must walk the pages down — but this call site did not certify {scratch.name} free, and the walk has no other register to use. Reserve it in page-sized steps, or free a scratch register at that site and certify it.")
		end 'noScratch'

		encodeStackProbe(code, stackSize: stackSize)
		return
	end 'walk'

	if stackSize > 0 'reserve'
		encodeRspAdjust(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, amount: stackSize)
	end 'reserve'
end 'encodePrologue'

// Open a frame LARGER than one page by touching every page on the way down, so the guard page is
// hit in order and the OS commits the stack a page at a time:
//
//     mov  r11, rsp          ; r11 = the frame's lowest address
//     sub  r11, stackSize
//   walk:
//     sub  rsp, 4096         ; rsp IS the cursor — no second register to find
//     mov  [rsp], r11        ; TOUCH. The value is irrelevant; the ACCESS is the point
//     cmp  rsp, r11
//     ja   walk
//     mov  rsp, r11          ; land exactly on the frame base
//
// ⭐ WHY rsp WALKS rather than a second scratch register (the shape both references use — the
// bootstrap's `__chkstk` cursors r11 against a limit in r10): a cursor plus a limit is TWO dead
// registers, and this is emitted INLINE at a function entry where certifying one is already the
// delicate half. Walking rsp itself needs exactly one, and it pays for itself twice — the final
// `mov rsp, r11` IS the reservation, so there is no separate `sub rsp, stackSize` to keep in step
// with the walk.
//
// ⚠ The last iteration may touch up to one page BELOW the frame base (the walk overshoots whenever
// the frame is not a whole number of pages) — the same overshoot the bootstrap's loop makes. It is
// harmless and it is not the bug this code exists to prevent: at the instant of that store rsp
// points AT the word being written, so nothing else owns it, and the only consequence is that the
// OS commits one page the function will not use. Skipping a page costs a fault; touching a spare
// one costs a page.
//
// ⚠ EMITTED ON EVERY x64 OS, NOT ONLY WINDOWS. Linux's default guard gap is 256 pages, so a frame
// of this size is already safe there and this walk is redundant on that lane — but touching pages
// you are about to write is never WRONG, the encoder has no target OS in hand (threading one down
// to the byte layer would be a new fact to keep in step for a sequence that costs nothing), and it
// is exactly what `-fstack-clash-protection` emits for the same instruction stream. The cost is
// borne only by a function whose frame is past a page, of which the entire spec corpus has three.
function encodeStackProbe(code ByteArray, stackSize StackSize)
	// The frame base, computed exactly as `emitGtStackGuard` computes its projected rsp — one
	// idiom for "rsp minus a frame", not two. The cast bounds the frame at i32.max, which is the
	// same bound `sub rsp, imm32` carries.
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: StackProbeScratchReg, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: StackProbeScratchReg, imm: stackSize as Imm32)

	let walkStart = code.count()
	encodeRspAdjust(code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, amount: StackGuardPageBytes)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: 0, srcReg: StackProbeScratchReg)
	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsp, src: StackProbeScratchReg, wide: true)
	// UNSIGNED `ja`: these are addresses, and a stack near the top of the address space would read
	// as negative under a signed compare.
	encodeJccBackward(code, cond: X64CondCode.above, targetOffset: walkStart, what: PatchSite.x64StackProbeRel32)

	encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsp, src: StackProbeScratchReg, wide: true)
end 'encodeStackProbe'

// epilogue: release the frame with `add rsp, stackSize` (mirror of prologue).
export function encodeEpilogue(code ByteArray, stackSize StackSize)
	if stackSize > 0 'release'
		encodeRspAdjust(code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, amount: stackSize)
	end 'release'
end 'encodeEpilogue'

// call rel32 to a named function. The 4-byte displacement is left zero and a
// CHUNK-LOCAL CallFixup records the disp32 offset; concat rebases it and resolves
// it to the callee's placement.
export function encodeCallDirect(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, targetName ByteArray)
	code.push(OpcodeCallRel32)
	callFixups.push(CallFixup.create(code.count(), targetName: targetName))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeCallDirect'

// `call r/m64` through a register — group-5 `/2`. The ONE home of the call-through-register
// byte shape, shared by the `callReg` TargetOp arm (a `callIndirect`'s colored callee) and the
// GT trampoline chunk, which calls the spawned function through a pointer held in a register.
// REX.W is ignored for a near call in long mode, so `48 FF D0` decodes as `call rax`.
export function encodeCallReg(code ByteArray, reg X64Register)
	encodeModRmExtDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCallIndirect, ext: Grp5ExtCall, rmField: reg)
end 'encodeCallReg'

// `call qword [base + disp]` through a memory operand — group-5 `/2`, mod=01/10 (P1.7a slice 2a). The
// memory twin of `encodeCallReg`, and the whole point of the fused witness call: the callee pointer is read
// from `[witnessAddr + slotOffset]` BY THE CALL and never occupies a register. REX.W is ignored for a near
// call, so `wide: false` keeps the prefix minimal (only a REX.B for an r8-r15 base); the `/2` extension
// rides the ModR/M reg field via `emitBaseDispModRmBits`, and `regField: rax` gives REX.R = 0 (an opcode
// extension needs none), so a base in rax..rdi emits no REX at all (`call [rax+8]` = `FF 50 08`).
function encodeCallMem(code ByteArray, base X64Register, disp Imm32)
	emitRex(code, wide: false, regField: X64Register.rax, rmField: base)
	code.push(OpcodeCallIndirect)
	emitBaseDispModRmBits(code, regFieldBits: Grp5ExtCall, base: base, disp: disp)
end 'encodeCallMem'

// call [rip+disp32] through an IAT slot. The disp32 is left zero and an IAT
// Relocation records its offset + slot; the PE writer patches it to the IAT
// entry's RVA once section layout is fixed.
export function encodeIatCall(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, slotIndex RelocSlotIndex)
	code.push(OpcodeCallIndirect)
	emitModRmRipRel(code, regFieldBits: Grp5ExtCall)
	iatRelocs.push(Relocation.create(code.count(), slotIndex: slotIndex))
	writeDword(code, value: 0)
end 'encodeIatCall'

// `mov gs:[disp32], srcReg` (64-bit) — a store to an ABSOLUTE address in the GS segment. On Win64 GS points at
// the Thread Information Block, so this writes NT_TIB.StackBase (disp 0x08) or StackLimit (disp 0x10) — the GT
// syscall shim's TIB repoint. Encoding: the GS prefix, REX.W (+REX.R for an r8-r15 source), `89 /r`, a ModR/M
// of mod=00 reg=src rm=100 (a SIB follows), a SIB of scale=1 index=none base=disp32, then the disp32. It cannot
// ride `encodeStoreBaseDispReg` (which addresses `[base + disp]`, a register base): the `[disp32]` no-base form
// needs the SIB with base=101, and the segment override has no home in that encoder. `emitRex`'s `rmField` is
// `rax` so REX.B stays clear — in this SIB form the base is a disp32, not a register, so REX.B must not be set.
export function encodeMovGsAbsReg(code ByteArray, disp Imm32, srcReg X64Register)
	code.push(GsSegmentPrefix)
	emitRex(code, wide: true, regField: srcReg, rmField: X64Register.rax)
	code.push(OpcodeMovRmReg)
	code.push(ModRmMod00 or (regLow3(srcReg) shl 3) or ModRmRmSibFollows)
	code.push(sibByte(MemoryIndexScale.byOne, indexBits: SibNoIndexField, baseBits: SibBaseDisp32Field))
	writeImm32Signed(code, value: disp)
end 'encodeMovGsAbsReg'

// `mov destReg, gs:[disp32]` (64-bit) — a LOAD from an absolute GS-segment address, the read twin of
// `encodeMovGsAbsReg`. The GT context switch reads the current NT_TIB StackBase/StackLimit through it to save
// them into the suspending GT before repointing the TIB at the resumed GT's stack, so the TIB always describes
// the stack actually executing. Same addressing form as the store (only the direction opcode differs, `89`→
// `8B`); `emitRex`'s `rmField` is `rax` so REX.B stays clear for the SIB disp32-base with no register base.
export function encodeMovRegGsAbs(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, disp Imm32)
	code.push(GsSegmentPrefix)
	emitRex(code, wide: true, regField: dest, rmField: X64Register.rax)
	code.push(OpcodeMovRegRm)
	code.push(ModRmMod00 or (regLow3(dest) shl 3) or ModRmRmSibFollows)
	code.push(sibByte(MemoryIndexScale.byOne, indexBits: SibNoIndexField, baseBits: SibBaseDisp32Field))
	writeImm32Signed(code, value: disp)
end 'encodeMovRegGsAbs'

// `mov destReg, gs:[baseReg]` (64-bit) — a LOAD from a GS-segment address held in a REGISTER, which is what
// makes a TLS slot readable in two loads and no call: the caller has the precomputed `index * 8 + 0x1480` in
// `baseReg` and this dereferences it. The GT syscall shim and every current-GT read in the scheduler go
// through it, so it is the hottest instruction the multi-M substrate has.
//
// ⚠ **IT DELEGATES ITS ModR/M TO `encodeModRmBaseDisp`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF ITS CORRECTNESS.** A hand-
// assembled `mod=00 | reg | rm=base` byte — which is what both reference compilers write — encodes `[rbp]`
// and `[r13]` as RIP-RELATIVE, reading a constant out of the code stream instead of the thread's slot. v1
// panics rather than emit it and the C# bootstrap avoids it by pinning one register; the shared assembler
// already answers it for every base (a zero-`disp8` for rbp/r13, a SIB for rsp/r12), so this encoder inherits
// the fix instead of restating it and the register allocator is left free to colour the operand anywhere.
//
// ⚠ The segment prefix must come BEFORE the REX byte — REX is required to be the last prefix — which is why
// the push order here is not the one `encodeModRmBaseDisp` would produce on its own; the prefix is emitted
// here and the rest delegated.
export function encodeMovRegGsBase(code ByteArray, dest X64Register, base X64Register)
	code.push(GsSegmentPrefix)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeMovRegRm, regField: dest, base: base, disp: 0, wide: true)
end 'encodeMovRegGsBase'

// A raw Linux x86-64 supervisor call: `mov eax, number` then `syscall` (0F 05). The x86-64 Linux ABI
// puts the number in eax/rax and arguments in rdi/rsi/rdx/r10/r8/r9 (which the isel pre-moved, exactly
// as it pre-moves a call's arguments), and returns the result in rax. Exported so the Linux entry stub
// (`BackendDispatch.prependLinuxEntryStub`) and the OS-primitive lowerings emit their syscalls through
// the one primitive — the twin of `arm64EmitSyscall` (`movz x8`/`svc`).
//
// The number rides the shared `encodeMovRegImm32`, whose `writeDword` carries the whole 32-bit value,
// so every in-use Linux syscall number (all far below u32.max) reaches eax intact; the zero-extending
// `mov r32` clears the high half exactly as the ABI wants the number to appear in rax.
export function encodeX64Syscall(code ByteArray, number MachineWord)
	encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: number)
	code.push(TwoByteOpcodeEscape)
	code.push(OpcodeSyscallSecondByte)
end 'encodeX64Syscall'

// ==========================================================================
// F2: the atomics, and the Windows per-thread identity word
// ==========================================================================

// `lock <op> [addrReg], srcReg` — the ONE byte shape all three x64 atomics share: the LOCK prefix, then
// an ordinary `r/m64, r64` memory form whose r/m is a bare `[base]`. `cmpxchg` and `xadd` live behind the
// two-byte escape and `add` does not, which is the whole of `twoByteEscape`.
//
// ⭐ It goes through `encodeModRmBaseDisp` rather than assembling its own ModR/M, which is what buys the
// rsp/r12 SIB and rbp/r13 zero-disp8 traps for free — an address operand coloured into rbp would
// otherwise encode as RIP-RELATIVE and read a constant from the code stream instead of the word the
// program named. A displacement of 0 is the only one these ops have: an atomic names an ADDRESS, and
// `StdOp.atomicCas`/`atomicRmw` carry no field offset (unlike `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect`).
//
// ⚠ **THE LOCK PREFIX IS NOT DECORATION AND IT IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION TO DROP.** Without it `xadd`/`add`
// are read-modify-write sequences another core can interleave with, and `cmpxchg`'s compare-and-store is
// not one indivisible step. On a single-core machine the difference is invisible; the day multi-M lands it
// is a lost counter or a lost queue node.
function encodeLockedMemReg(code ByteArray, twoByteEscape bool, opcode MachineWord, addrReg X64Register, srcReg X64Register)
	code.push(LockPrefix)
	encodeModRmBaseDisp(code, twoByteEscape: twoByteEscape, opcode: opcode, regField: srcReg, base: addrReg, disp: AtomicWordDisp, wide: true)
end 'encodeLockedMemReg'

// Resolve a colored operand to its physical register. A virtual reg here is an
// allocator bug (register allocation must run before emission).
//
// ⚠⚠ IT ASKS NO QUESTION ABOUT THE REGISTER'S FILE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS NOT THE DOOR A
// FIXED-CLASS ENCODER USES. See `resolveGpr` — hand a raw result of this to a GPR-only
// encoder and an XMM assembles as the GPR sharing its low nibble, silently. Use it directly
// ONLY where the op is genuinely class-agnostic and the CLASS ITSELF is what is being
// dispatched on (`emitRegRegMove`, `emitWord64Load`/`Store`, `emitRegRegSwap`).
function resolvePhysical(vreg TargetVReg) returns X64Register
	match vreg 'resolve'
		physical(reg) then return try X64Register.fromRawValue(reg) otherwise panic("x64 emitter: colored register number {reg} is not a valid X64Register — the allocator assigned a register outside the x64 file")
		virtual(id) then panic("x64 emitter: unresolved virtual register v{id} — register allocation did not color it before emission")
	end 'resolve'
end 'resolvePhysical'

// ⚠⚠ THE CLASS-CHECKING DOOR — THE PRICE OF THE ONE-ENUM DESIGN, AND PAYING IT IS NOT OPTIONAL.
//
// Every encoder in this file takes a register's low 3 bits and emits an instruction whose
// OPCODE — not whose operand — decides which file those bits name. `X64Register` deliberately
// puts `xmm3` at rawValue 19, whose low nibble is 3: the same 3 as `rbx`. So handing
// `encodeStoreBaseDispReg` an xmm3 assembles `mov [rsp+disp], rbx` — a spill of a register
// nobody named, with no error at any layer and no crash to find it by, just a wrong answer.
//
// Routing every FIXED-CLASS encoder through this turns that entire class of mistake into a
// LOUD compiler bug naming the op that caused it. The uniformity the one enum buys everywhere
// above the encoder is paid for here, at the one place where a bare register number is
// genuinely ambiguous. Class-agnostic ops do not come through here — they dispatch on
// `registerClass` instead, which is the same fact used rather than asserted.
function requireClass(reg X64Register, class RegClass) returns X64Register
	if registerClass(reg) != class 'wrongFile'
		panic("x64 emitter: {reg.name} is in the {registerClass(reg).name} register file where the {class.name} file is required — this encoding would take its low 3 bits and silently name a register in the wrong file (see TargetOp's SSE band)")
	end 'wrongFile'
	return reg
end 'requireClass'

// Resolve a colored operand to a physical GPR, panicking if it is an XMM.
function resolveGpr(vreg TargetVReg) returns X64Register
	return requireClass(resolvePhysical(vreg), class: RegClass.gpr)
end 'resolveGpr'

// Resolve a colored operand to a physical XMM, panicking if it is a GPR — `resolveGpr`'s
// mirror, and it exists for the same reason in the other direction: `addsd` would encode
// `rbx` as `xmm3` just as happily.
function resolveXmm(vreg TargetVReg) returns X64Register
	return requireClass(resolvePhysical(vreg), class: RegClass.xmm)
end 'resolveXmm'

// The `[rsp + disp]` displacement of spill slot `slot`: the function's spill-area base
// (below which sit shadow space + outgoing args on a caller — `spillSlotBaseDisp`) plus
// `slot * SpillSlotBytes`. Named so the frame-slot arithmetic is spelled once. The cast is
// safe: shv2's frames are far below i32.max, and the splitter caps slot count at valueCount.
function slotDisplacement(spillBaseDisp StackSize, slot SpillSlot) returns Imm32
	return (spillBaseDisp + slot * SpillSlotBytes) as Imm32
end 'slotDisplacement'

// Encode one TargetOp into `code`, recording call / IAT / block-jump fixups as it
// goes. `rel32Fixups` collects intra-chunk branch fixups the chunk resolves once
// every block's offset is known (see resolveChunkRel32Fixups). `spillBaseDisp` is the
// function's spill-area base offset from rsp (see spillSlotBaseDisp), added to each
// spill/reload slot displacement.
function emitTargetOp(op TargetOp, code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, rel32Fixups ChunkRel32FixupArray, jumpTables JumpTablePendingArray, spillBaseDisp StackSize, usesGt bool)
	match op 'emit'
		// `probeScratchIsFree` is certified here and NOWHERE ELSE. Both minters of this op stand at
		// a function ENTRY — `insertPrologueAtEntry` (X64PrologueEpilogue) and `prependEntryStub`'s
		// `mrt_start` (BackendDispatch) — so the only live values are the incoming ARGUMENT
		// registers, and `StackProbeScratchReg` is not one in either ABI. The GT stack guard
		// emitted immediately above this already clobbers it, which would be a live bug if
		// anything held it. This is also the ONLY prologue whose frame is unbounded (`mrt_start`'s
		// is a fixed 0x20).
		prologue(stackSize) then encodePrologue(code, stackSize: stackSize, probeScratchIsFree: true)
		epilogue(stackSize) then encodeEpilogue(code, stackSize: stackSize)
		ret then code.push(OpcodeRet)
		jmp(targetBlockId) then encodeJmp(code, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		jcc(cond, targetBlockId) then encodeJcc(code, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups, cond: cond, targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		movRegImm(dest, value) then encodeMovRegImm64(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), value: value)
		movRegImm32(dest, value) then encodeMovRegImm32(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), value: value)
		// CLASS-AGNOSTIC: a move is a move whichever file it names, so the instruction is chosen
		// from the resolved registers rather than from the op (see `emitRegRegMove`).
		movRegReg(destReg, srcReg) then emitRegRegMove(code, dest: resolvePhysical(destReg), src: resolvePhysical(srcReg))
		movReg32Reg32(destReg, srcReg) then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: resolveGpr(destReg), src: resolveGpr(srcReg), wide: false)
		subRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveGpr(rhs), wide: true)
		imulRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeImulRegReg(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveGpr(rhs))
		negReg(dest, _) then encodeGrp3Reg(code, ext: Grp3ExtNeg, reg: resolveGpr(dest))
		cmpRegReg(lhsReg, rhsReg) then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: resolveGpr(lhsReg), src: resolveGpr(rhsReg), wide: true)
		leaRegRegReg(dest, base, index) then encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), base: resolveGpr(base), index: resolveGpr(index), scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
		leaRegRegImm32(dest, base, disp) then encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), base: resolveGpr(base), disp: disp)
		imulRegRegImm32(dest, src, imm) then encodeImulRegRegImm32(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveGpr(src), imm: imm)
		cmpRegImm32(reg, imm) then encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: resolveGpr(reg), imm: imm)
		// CLASS-AGNOSTIC: `xchg` for a GPR pair, a triple-`xorpd` for an XMM pair — x64 has no
		// `xchg xmm, xmm` at all (see `emitRegRegSwap`).
		xchgRegReg(a, b) then emitRegRegSwap(code, a: resolvePhysical(a), b: resolvePhysical(b))
		callDirect(targetName) then encodeCallDirect(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: targetName)
		// In a GT-scheduler module every Win32 IAT call is wrapped in the system-stack shim (P1.5-B1a′): a GT
		// stack is too small for the kernel's deep usage, so the call runs on the global system stack. The shim
		// is a runtime no-op off a GT stack (`currentGt == 0 || stackBase == 0` → direct), so wrapping every call
		// is correct; a non-GT module lays out no `__sched_tls_teb_offset` to reference, so it stays a bare `iatCall`.
		iatCall(slotIndex) then emitGtGatedIatCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, slotIndex: slotIndex, usesGt: usesGt)
		cqo then encodeCqo(code)
		// The two group-3 divides differ in the /ext field and in nothing else — same F7 opcode, same
		// implicit RDX:RAX pair — which is why one op with a signedness field encodes both.
		divideReg(divisor, signedness) then encodeGrp3Reg(code, ext: Grp3ExtIdiv if signedness == IntegerSignedness.signed else Grp3ExtDiv, reg: resolveGpr(divisor))
		pushReg(reg) then encodePushReg(code, reg: requireClass(reg, class: RegClass.gpr))
		popReg(reg) then encodePopReg(code, reg: requireClass(reg, class: RegClass.gpr))
		// CLASS-AGNOSTIC: a spill slot is 8 bytes and an f64 is 8 bytes, so the SAME slot the
		// splitter already reserved holds either, and only the instruction differs.
		storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg) then emitWord64Store(code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: slotDisplacement(spillBaseDisp, slot: slot), src: resolvePhysical(srcReg))
		loadRegSlot(destReg, slot) then emitWord64Load(code, dest: resolvePhysical(destReg), base: X64Register.rsp, disp: slotDisplacement(spillBaseDisp, slot: slot))
		// The two-address word ALU ops. Their `dest` already HOLDS `lhs` by the time the
		// instruction runs (the allocator either coalesced the two into one register or
		// materialized `mov dest, lhs`), so — exactly as for `subRegReg` — the encoded
		// instruction names only `dest` and `rhs`, and `lhs` is deliberately ignored here.
		andRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeAndRmReg, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveGpr(rhs), wide: true)
		orRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeOrRmReg, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveGpr(rhs), wide: true)
		xorRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveGpr(rhs), wide: true)
		notReg(dest, _) then encodeGrp3Reg(code, ext: Grp3ExtNot, reg: resolveGpr(dest))
		andRegImm32(dest, _, imm) then encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtAnd, reg: resolveGpr(dest), imm: imm)
		orRegImm32(dest, _, imm) then encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtOr, reg: resolveGpr(dest), imm: imm)
		xorRegImm32(dest, _, imm) then encodeGrp1RegImm32(code, ext: Grp1ExtXor, reg: resolveGpr(dest), imm: imm)
		shlRegImm8(dest, _, imm) then encodeGrp2RegImm8(code, ext: Grp2ExtShl, reg: resolveGpr(dest), count: imm)
		sarRegImm8(dest, _, imm) then encodeGrp2RegImm8(code, ext: Grp2ExtSar, reg: resolveGpr(dest), count: imm)
		shrRegImm8(dest, _, imm) then encodeGrp2RegImm8(code, ext: Grp2ExtShr, reg: resolveGpr(dest), count: imm)
		// The variable-count shifts read their count from CL, which the lowering has already
		// loaded — so, like the immediate forms, the encoded instruction names only `dest`.
		shlRegCl(dest, _) then encodeGrp2RegCl(code, ext: Grp2ExtShl, reg: resolveGpr(dest))
		sarRegCl(dest, _) then encodeGrp2RegCl(code, ext: Grp2ExtSar, reg: resolveGpr(dest))
		shrRegCl(dest, _) then encodeGrp2RegCl(code, ext: Grp2ExtShr, reg: resolveGpr(dest))
		setccReg(dest, cond) then encodeSetccReg(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), cond: cond)
		// The data-section band. Each dispatches to an encoder that ALREADY EXISTED — the
		// `[base+disp]` load/store/movzx forms the spill/reload path uses, here with a global's
		// address in the base register instead of rsp. No second encoder is minted for them.
		leaRegGlobal(dest, label) then encodeLeaRegGlobal(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: resolveGpr(dest), label: label)
		// `base` is ALWAYS a GPR — an address is an integer, and RIP-relative `lea` put it there.
		// `dest`/`src` are class-dispatched at word64 (an f64 global moves through `movsd`), which
		// is why they resolve without a class assertion. See `emitDataLoad`.
		loadRegBaseDisp(dest, base, disp, width) then emitDataLoad(code, dest: resolvePhysical(dest), base: resolveGpr(base), disp: disp, width: width)
		storeBaseDispReg(base, disp, src, width) then emitDataStore(code, base: resolveGpr(base), disp: disp, src: resolvePhysical(src), width: width)
		// --- The SSE band (P1.0d.4) ---
		//
		// Every arm resolves through `resolveXmm`/`resolveGpr` rather than `resolvePhysical`: these
		// are FIXED-CLASS encodings, and a bare register number is ambiguous at exactly this point
		// (see `requireClass`). The two converts and the bitcast are the dialect's only cross-class
		// ops, and they are the only arms here naming both doors — which is the class change made
		// visible.
		addsdRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeAddsd, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(rhs), wide: false)
		subsdRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeSubsd, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(rhs), wide: false)
		mulsdRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeMulsd, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(rhs), wide: false)
		divsdRegReg(dest, _, rhs) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeDivsd, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(rhs), wide: false)
		// `ucomisd lhs, rhs` — the reg field is `lhs`, so a following `ja` reads "lhs above rhs".
		// The 66 prefix, not F2: `ucomisd` is the packed-double-prefixed form.
		ucomisdRegReg(lhs, rhs) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodeUcomisd, dest: resolveXmm(lhs), src: resolveXmm(rhs), wide: false)
		movsdRegRip(dest, label) then encodeMovsdRegRip(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: resolveXmm(dest), label: label)
		// REX.W is what makes these the 64-bit converts — the ONE place `wide: true` is correct
		// for an SSE instruction, because here it selects the GPR half's width rather than an
		// operand size the mandatory prefix already chose.
		cvtsi2sdRegReg(dest, src) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeCvtsi2sd, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveGpr(src), wide: true)
		cvttsd2siRegReg(dest, src) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeCvttsd2si, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveXmm(src), wide: true)
		// The MATH INTRINSICS (P1.0d.4-C). `sqrtsd` is an ordinary two-byte SSE form and rides the
		// shared `encodeSseRegReg` with only its opcode changed; `roundsd` is three-byte-plus-imm8
		// and has its own encoder (see `encodeRoundsdRegReg`).
		sqrtsdRegReg(dest, src) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: OpcodeSqrtsd, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(src), wide: false)
		roundsdRegReg(dest, src, mode) then encodeRoundsdRegReg(code, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(src), mode: mode)
		// The BIT REINTERPRETATION. The THIRD arm naming both class doors — and, unlike the two
		// converts, its ModR/M fields are INVERTED, which `encodeMovqGprXmm` owns so that this arm
		// can read in the direction the data actually flows.
		movqGprXmm(dest, src) then encodeMovqGprXmm(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), src: resolveXmm(src))
		// The INVERSE reinterpretation, and the arm that shows the pair is asymmetric only in its
		// ENCODING: this direction's ModR/M fields are the ordinary `r, r/m` mapping, so it rides the
		// shared `encodeSseRegReg` with `wide: true` — the same `true` the two converts need, and for
		// the same reason (it selects the GPR half's width, not an operand size the 66 already chose).
		movqXmmGpr(dest, src) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.packedDouble, opcode: OpcodeMovqXmmRm, dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveGpr(src), wide: true)
		// ⚠ THE ONE ARM HERE THAT EMITS TWO INSTRUCTIONS, and `src` is deliberately unused: the op is
		// two-address (a REUSE def — see TargetOperands), so by the time coloring is done `dest` and
		// `src` ARE the same register and the shifts work on it in place. Naming `src` in the encoding
		// would be a second, redundant claim about a fact the allocator has already established, and
		// the two could only ever disagree.
		absF64RegReg(dest, _) then encodeAbsF64Reg(code, reg: resolveXmm(dest))
		// The sign-bit FLIP: three instructions, `src` unused for `absF64RegReg`'s reason (a REUSE def —
		// coloring has already made `dest` and `src` one register).
		negF64RegReg(dest, _) then encodeNegF64Reg(code, reg: resolveXmm(dest))
		// The `.rdata` address `lea` (P1.2 wave A) — `leaRegGlobal`'s twin, GPR dest, its label filed
		// as an `.rdata` relocation. See `encodeLeaRegRdata`.
		leaRegRdata(dest, label) then encodeLeaRegRdata(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: resolveGpr(dest), label: label)
		// `minsd`/`maxsd` are two-address SSE forms like `addsd`, so `lhs` is deliberately unused for the
		// same reason `absF64RegReg` ignores its `src`: the REUSE def means coloring has already made
		// `dest` and `lhs` the same register, and naming `lhs` here would be a second claim about a fact
		// the allocator established. `rhs` is the encoded source — and it is the operand both
		// instructions return on an unordered compare, which is what makes the NaN behaviour come out
		// right. Only the opcode BYTE distinguishes them (`minMaxSdOpcode`).
		minMaxSdRegReg(dest, _, rhs, kind) then encodeSseRegReg(code, prefix: SseMandatoryPrefix.scalarDouble, opcode: minMaxSdOpcode(kind), dest: resolveXmm(dest), src: resolveXmm(rhs), wide: false)
		// The arm64 band is encoded by the arm64 backend (`Arm64Backend`, wave 1), never here — the
		// x64 encoder only ever sees x64 ops (`buildBackend` dispatches on `target.cpu`). The
		// band-append invariant forced this arm; a panic keeps the ISA boundary loud.
		arm64Ret to arm64Fcsel then panic("emitTargetOp: an arm64 op reached the x64 encoder — the arm64 backend (wave 1) encodes the arm64 band")
		// The dense-match jump table: the full cmp/jae/lea/movsxd/lea/jmp sequence, its default-edge rel32
		// and table-address rdata reloc filed inline, its per-slot block-address relocs recorded in
		// `jumpTables` (resolved after the block loop). The arm64 jump table never reaches the x64 encoder.
		x64JumpTable(indexReg, defaultTarget, caseTargets, tableLabel) then encodeX64JumpTable(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups, jumpTables: jumpTables, indexReg: resolveGpr(indexReg), defaultTarget: defaultTarget, caseTargets: caseTargets, tableLabel: tableLabel)
		arm64JumpTable then panic("emitTargetOp: an arm64 jump table reached the x64 encoder — the arm64 backend encodes `arm64JumpTable`")
		// F1b: the byte-fill size ladder. Its two operands are already in rdi/rcx (the isel's pre-moves), so
		// the op carries only the broadcast pattern — see `encodeX64MemFill`.
		x64MemFill(fillByte) then encodeX64MemFill(code, fillByte: fillByte)
		arm64MemFill then panic("emitTargetOp: an arm64 fill ladder reached the x64 encoder — the arm64 backend encodes `arm64MemFill`")
		// P1.5-A1: a function's address (`lea dest, [rip+disp32]` + a `.text` CallFixup — the existing
		// `encodeLeaRegFuncAddr`, until now called only by the runtime stubs), and a register-indirect
		// call (`FF /2`, mod=11 — `call reg`). `encodeModRmExtDirect` emits `REX.W FF /2`; REX.W is IGNORED
		// for a near call in long mode (the operand size is forced to 64-bit), so `48 FF D0` decodes as
		// `call rax` and REX.B still selects r8..r15 — the same reg-direct group-5 encoding `iatCall` uses
		// in its RIP-relative form.
		leaRegFunc(dest, funcName) then encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(code, callFixups: callFixups, dest: resolveGpr(dest), targetName: funcName)
		callReg(reg) then encodeCallReg(code, reg: resolveGpr(reg))
		// x64-linux: a raw `syscall`. The number is the op's immediate (`mov eax, number`); its arguments
		// were pre-moved into rdi/rsi/rdx/r10/r8/r9 by the OS-primitive lowering, exactly as a call's are.
		x64Syscall(number) then encodeX64Syscall(code, number: number)
		// P1.7a slice 2a: the fused witness call's memory-indirect `call qword [baseReg + disp]` — the callee
		// pointer read from the witness table by the call itself, never virtualized.
		callMem(baseReg, disp) then encodeCallMem(code, base: resolveGpr(baseReg), disp: disp)
		// x64 places its own stack arguments through `storeBaseDispReg`/`loadRegBaseDisp` with an rsp/rbp base
		// (see the dialect header for why arm64 cannot), so neither arm64 stack-argument op is ever produced
		// for an x64 program and reaching this encoder is a lowering bug, not a missing encoding.
		arm64StoreOutgoingArg to arm64LoadIncomingArg then panic("emitX64TargetOp: an arm64 stack-argument op reached the x64 encoder — x64 places stack arguments with `storeBaseDispReg`/`loadRegBaseDisp` off rsp/rbp")
		// x64's own bit reinterpretations are `movqGprXmm`/`movqXmmGpr`, encoded above; neither arm64
		// `fmov` general↔vector form ever reaches this encoder (`buildBackend` dispatches on
		// `target.cpu`). ONE arm for the pair — the reason is the ISA, not the direction.
		arm64FmovGprFp to arm64FmovFpGpr then panic("emitX64TargetOp: an arm64-only general↔vector `fmov` reached the x64 encoder — x64's bit reinterpretations are `movqGprXmm`/`movqXmmGpr`")
		// `lea destReg, [rsp + slotDisp]` — the ADDRESS of a frame slot, through the SAME
		// `slotDisplacement` the spill/reload pair above resolve their slots with, so a promoted record and
		// a spill can never disagree about where slot `k` is.
		leaRegSlot(destReg, slot) then encodeLeaRegRegImm32(code, dest: resolveGpr(destReg), base: X64Register.rsp, disp: slotDisplacement(spillBaseDisp, slot: slot))
		// F2 — the three locked memory forms and the TEB read. The CAS's comparand is NOT an operand here:
		// x86 pins it to RAX, and the isel's `movRegReg rax, expected` put it there (see
		// `TargetDialect.x64LockCmpxchg`'s implicit pair).
		x64LockCmpxchg(addrReg, srcReg) then encodeLockedMemReg(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeCmpxchgRmReg, addrReg: resolveGpr(addrReg), srcReg: resolveGpr(srcReg))
		// `lock xadd`'s register operand is BOTH its addend and its result, so the byte shape names `dest` —
		// which the reuse-def has already put in `src`'s register.
		x64LockXadd(dest, _, addrReg) then encodeLockedMemReg(code, twoByteEscape: true, opcode: OpcodeXaddRmReg, addrReg: resolveGpr(addrReg), srcReg: resolveGpr(dest))
		x64LockAdd(addrReg, srcReg) then encodeLockedMemReg(code, twoByteEscape: false, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, addrReg: resolveGpr(addrReg), srcReg: resolveGpr(srcReg))
		// The TEB self-pointer read IS `encodeMovRegGsAbs` at the self-pointer's offset — the SAME absolute
		// GS form the GT context switch reads NT_TIB StackBase/StackLimit through, so the segment-addressing
		// bytes (prefix, REX, the no-base SIB) are stated once rather than a second time here.
		x64TebSelfPointer(dest) then encodeMovRegGsAbs(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), disp: WindowsTebSelfPointerOffset)
		x64GsLoadRegBase(dest, base) then encodeMovRegGsBase(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), base: resolveGpr(base))
		// The arm64 atomics never reach this encoder (`buildBackend` dispatches on `target.cpu`), exactly as
		// the arm64 `fmov` pair above does not — x64's own atomics are the three locked forms one line up.
		arm64AtomicCas to arm64ThreadPointer then panic("emitX64TargetOp: an arm64-only atomic or thread-pointer read reached the x64 encoder — x64's atomics are `x64LockCmpxchg`/`x64LockXadd`/`x64LockAdd` and its thread token is `x64TebSelfPointer`")
		// --- EC16: the scaled-index band ---
		//
		// ⭐ ALL THREE GO THROUGH THE ENCODERS THE `[base + disp]` BAND ALREADY USES, with the index
		// riding in the `index`/`indexScale` pair every one of them already takes. So the width
		// decision, the register-file dispatch (an f64 element loads through `movsd`), the rbp/r13
		// zero-disp8 trap and the rsp-index panic are all answered ONCE, for the indexed forms and the
		// unindexed ones together.
		//
		// `base` and `index` are ALWAYS GPRs — an address is an integer — while `dest`/`src` are
		// class-dispatched at word64, exactly as `loadRegBaseDisp`/`storeBaseDispReg` are.
		leaRegBaseIndexScale(dest, base, index, scale) then encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(code, dest: resolveGpr(dest), base: resolveGpr(base), index: resolveGpr(index), scale: scale)
		loadRegBaseIndexScale(dest, base, index, scale, disp, width) then emitDataLoad(code, dest: resolvePhysical(dest), base: resolveGpr(base), disp: disp, width: width, index: x64SibIndex(resolveGpr(index)), indexScale: scale)
		storeBaseIndexScaleReg(base, index, scale, disp, src, width) then emitDataStore(code, base: resolveGpr(base), disp: disp, src: resolvePhysical(src), width: width, index: x64SibIndex(resolveGpr(index)), indexScale: scale)
		arm64AddLsl then panic("emitX64TargetOp: an arm64 shifted-register `add` reached the x64 encoder — x64 spells a scaled-index address as `leaRegBaseIndexScale`")
		// EC18 — the ONE-operand `imul r/m64` (`REX.W F7 /5`): RDX:RAX ⟸ RAX × src. The same group-3
		// encoder the two divides use, at a different extension, which is the whole difference between
		// the four one-operand forms this family has.
		imulHighReg(src) then encodeGrp3Reg(code, ext: Grp3ExtImul, reg: resolveGpr(src))
		arm64Fneg then panic("emitTargetOp: an arm64 `fneg` reached the x64 encoder — the arm64 backend encodes the arm64 band; x64 spells the flip as `negF64RegReg`")
	end 'emit'
end 'emitTargetOp'

// ==========================================================================
// Per-function chunk emission
// ==========================================================================

// Emit one function into a FunctionCodeChunk with CHUNK-LOCAL fixup offsets. The
// caller (concat) shifts every inter-function fixup (call/IAT) by the chunk's start
// offset. Intra-function block branches (M4's `jmp`/`jcc`), by contrast, are resolved
// HERE: a function's blocks are contiguous within its one chunk, so a block branch's
// rel32 is chunk-local and independent of where concat places the chunk. Each block's
// chunk-local start offset is recorded as it is emitted, then `resolveChunkRel32Fixups`
// patches every pending branch (including forward jumps to blocks emitted later).
// `usesGt` (whether the program carries the GT scheduler) is passed in — it is `RuntimeUsage.usesGt`, the
// bit `installGtRuntime` was gated on, read once by the caller and never re-derived per function, so this
// stays O(1) per function. It gates the Win32 syscall shim on every `iatCall` (see `emitGtGatedIatCall`)
// and the per-function morestack guard below.
export function emitFunctionChunk(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, usesGt bool) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()
	var iatRelocs = RelocationArray.create()
	// The chunk's code -> .data references, filed by `encodeLeaRegGlobal` with CHUNK-LOCAL offsets;
	// `concatX64FunctionChunks` rebases them exactly as it rebases `callFixups` and `iatRelocs`.
	var rdataRelocs = RdataRelocationArray.create()
	var rel32Fixups = ChunkRel32FixupArray.create()
	// Dense-match jump tables encoded in this chunk, awaiting their per-slot block-address relocations —
	// resolved after the block loop, when `chunkLabelOffsets` holds every block's chunk-local offset.
	var jumpTables = JumpTablePendingArray.create()
	// Sized from `func.nextBlockId` — the function's ids run 0..nextBlockId-1 — so the table is two
	// allocations and `place` never reallocates. See BlockKeyedTable for why this is an array and not a Map.
	var chunkLabelOffsets = BlockOffsetTable.forBlockCount(func.nextBlockId)

	// The base rsp offset of this function's spill-slot area, computed once (it depends
	// only on whether the function makes a call — a caller's slots sit ABOVE the Win64
	// shadow space so a callee never clobbers a spilled value). Every spill/reload adds
	// `slot * SpillSlotBytes` to it. See spillSlotBaseDisp for the frame-layout rationale.
	let spillBaseDisp = spillSlotBaseDisp(module, func: func)

	// ⭐ THE GT STACK GUARD (P1.5-B1a′). Emitted at the very START of the entry block — before `push rbp` — for a
	// USER function of a GT module that reserves a frame, so a green thread whose stack would overflow grows +
	// relocates it first. It is EXEMPT for the compiler's own SCAFFOLDING: the scheduler core must never trigger
	// the grower, and their kernel calls are already shimmed onto the system stack (slice 1). ⚠ The exemption is
	// asked of the FUNCTION's provenance, never of its NAME — a `__`-name test handed it to a user function
	// declared in `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` and SEGFAULTED a green thread; see `isRuntimeFunction`'s header for the
	// measurement. ⛔ And the question is `skipsGreenThreadStackGuard`, NOT `isRuntimeFunction`: a synthesized
	// function that runs USER CODE inside `main` (`__lazy_init#<label>`) satisfies the second and NEEDS the
	// guard — see that predicate for why the two came apart. Emitted here at the
	// byte level (not as a TargetOp), exactly like the syscall shim — so it is invisible to the Target-IR goldens
	// and verified by the RUN + disassembly instead. `computeGuardFrameBytes` is the SAME frame the prologue
	// reserves, so the guard projects rsp by exactly what the function consumes.
	// Only a GT module's non-runtime functions can be guarded, so `computeGuardFrameBytes` (which allocates a
	// callee-saved scan) is called ONLY for those — a non-GT module (the common case, and the whole scale corpus)
	// pays nothing here.
	let mightGuard = usesGt and not skipsGreenThreadStackGuard(func)
	var guardFrameBytes = 0 as StackSize
	if mightGuard 'guardFrame'
		guardFrameBytes = computeGuardFrameBytes(module, func: func)
	end 'guardFrame'
	let emitGuard = mightGuard and guardFrameBytes > 0
	var isFirstBlock = true

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("emitFunctionChunk: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		// Record this block's chunk-local start BEFORE emitting its ops, so branches
		// (from any block) can resolve to it. Blocks are emitted in blockRefs order.
		chunkLabelOffsets.place(block.id, value: code.count())

		if isFirstBlock and emitGuard 'guard'
			emitGtStackGuard(code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, frameBytes: guardFrameBytes)
		end 'guard'
		isFirstBlock = false

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("emitFunctionChunk: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			emitTargetOp(op, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups, jumpTables: jumpTables, spillBaseDisp: spillBaseDisp, usesGt: usesGt)
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			let termOp = try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("emitFunctionChunk: terminator not in module.ops")
			emitTargetOp(termOp, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups, jumpTables: jumpTables, spillBaseDisp: spillBaseDisp, usesGt: usesGt)
		end 'hasTerm'
	end 'eachBlock'

	resolveChunkRel32Fixups(code, labelOffsets: chunkLabelOffsets, rel32Fixups: rel32Fixups)

	// M1 registers no rdata constants (no float/mask islands) — those GROW in at
	// M3 (floats) / M10 (strings).
	var chunk = FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(func.name, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
	chunk.jumpTableRelocs = resolveJumpTableRelocs(jumpTables, blockOffsets: chunkLabelOffsets)
	return chunk
end 'emitFunctionChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// Concatenation: lay out .text, rebase fixups, resolve intra-module calls
// ==========================================================================

// The runtime entry point's function name — its `.text` offset becomes the PE
// AddressOfEntryPoint (see augmentWithRuntime, which lays it out first). Exported
// so augmentWithRuntime builds a function under the same name the concat looks up.
export let EntryPointName = b"mrt_start"

// `__symtable` — the backtrace's function-name table — is a FORMAT, not an x64 fact: its layout, its
// name and its serializer live in `Targets/Shared/BacktraceFormat` because the arm64 panic runtime
// reads the identical table. Concat below appends it to close `.text`.

// Concatenate every function chunk into the final `.text`, rebase each chunk's
// CHUNK-LOCAL fixups to absolute `.text` offsets, resolve intra-module direct
// calls to rel32, and package the result as a format-neutral CodeResult. Chunk
// iteration order (which augmentWithRuntime arranges so `mrt_start` is first)
// fixes the `.text` byte layout.
export function concatX64FunctionChunks(targetModule TargetModule, stdModule StdModule, chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns CodeResult throws CompileError
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var relocations = RelocationArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()
	var labelOffsets = FuncLabelOffsetMap.create()
	var debugSymbols = DebugSymbolArray.create()
	var rdataRelocations = RdataRelocationArray.create()
	var jumpTableRelocs = JumpTableRelocArray.create()

	// A label claimed twice is a wrong `.text` address, never a near miss — see `recordChunkLabel`, which
	// owns the rule and the refusal for both native backends.
	var labelsLaid = 0 as ChunkLabelCount
	for chunk in chunks 'eachChunk'
		let funcStart = code.count()
		try recordChunkLabel(labelOffsets, name: chunk.funcName, offset: funcStart, laidOut: labelsLaid, project: project, funcNames: funcNames)
		labelsLaid = labelsLaid + 1
		debugSymbols.push(DebugSymbol.create(chunk.funcName, codeOffset: funcStart))
		code.append(chunk.code)

		// Rebase chunk-local fixup offsets to absolute `.text` offsets.
		for fx in chunk.callFixups 'rebaseCalls'
			callFixups.push(CallFixup.create(funcStart + fx.codeOffset, targetName: fx.targetName))
		end 'rebaseCalls'
		for r in chunk.iatRelocs 'rebaseIat'
			relocations.push(Relocation.create(funcStart + r.codeOffset, slotIndex: r.slotIndex))
		end 'rebaseIat'
		// A code -> .data reference is rebased exactly as the two above are, and RESOLVED here to
		// its slot's offset within `.data`. The writer then adds the section's RVA — the only part
		// of the address the backend cannot know, because sections are not laid out yet.
		for rr in chunk.rdataRelocs 'rebaseRdata'
			var reloc = RdataRelocation.create(funcStart + rr.codeOffset, rdataLabel: rr.rdataLabel, kind: rr.kind)
			reloc.rdataOffset = sectionOffsetOfReloc(globalData, reloc: rr)
			rdataRelocations.push(reloc)
		end 'rebaseRdata'
		// Rebase each jump-table reloc's per-slot target offsets from chunk-local to whole-`.text` (the crux
		// step: a block's offset is `funcStart + its chunk-local offset`). The writer patches the `.rdata`
		// blob against these — the `Arm64FuncAdrpReloc.textOffset` rebase, per slot.
		for jt in chunk.jumpTableRelocs 'rebaseJumpTables'
			jumpTableRelocs.push(rebaseJumpTableReloc(jt, funcStart: funcStart))
		end 'rebaseJumpTables'
	end 'eachChunk'

	// `__symtable` CLOSES `.text`, after the last function chunk — the placement the panic
	// runtime's `textSize = __symtable - mrt_start` depends on. Appending it here, from the
	// loop's own output, is what makes that structural: every symbol's offset is known
	// because the loop just assigned it, and nothing can be laid out after it because
	// nothing follows. It is registered in `labelOffsets` (so the runtime's `lea rax, [rip
	// + __symtable]` resolves like any other chunk reference) but deliberately NOT in
	// `debugSymbols` — it is data, not a function, and an entry for it would let a walk
	// symbolize a stray return address INTO the table.
	// ⚠ It is DATA, not a function — which is why it has no chunk and no `debugSymbols` entry — but it does
	// CLAIM A NAME in this map, so it goes through the same door and is counted like any other label. It is
	// exempt from being a function, not from being unique: a second claim on `__symtable` would move the
	// bound `textSize = __symtable - mrt_start` reads, silently mis-sizing every backtrace.
	padTo(code, size: alignUp(code.count(), alignment: SymbolTableAlignment))
	try recordChunkLabel(labelOffsets, name: SymbolTableName, offset: code.count(), laidOut: labelsLaid, project: project, funcNames: funcNames)
	code.append(buildSymbolTableBytes(debugSymbols))

	resolveCallFixups(code, labelOffsets: labelOffsets, callFixups: callFixups)

	let entryOffset = try labelOffsets.get(EntryPointName) otherwise panic("concatX64FunctionChunks: '{String.from(EntryPointName)}' was not laid out — augmentWithRuntime did not run before emission")

	// The panic runtime uses `mrt_start`'s address as `text_base` and symbolizes a frame by
	// `addr - text_base`, so a function laid out BELOW it would produce a negative offset
	// and vanish from every backtrace without a word. `augmentWithRuntime` prepends the
	// stub to guarantee this; the guard is here, at the one place that decides the layout,
	// because "the entry stub is first" reads like a cosmetic property of a function list.
	if entryOffset != 0 'entryNotFirst'
		panic("concatX64FunctionChunks: '{String.from(EntryPointName)}' must be laid out FIRST in .text but is at offset {entryOffset} — the panic runtime takes its address as `text_base` and symbolizes every frame relative to it, so anything below it would silently drop out of all backtraces")
	end 'entryNotFirst'

	// symdata and covdata are still empty — they GROW back at their milestones (coverage, and the
	// backtrace's own table already closes `.text` instead). `.rdata` is NO LONGER among them:
	// P1.0d.4 fills it with the f64 constant islands `lowerFloatConst` registered, and
	// `rdataRelocations` carries the code -> .rdata references resolved above alongside the .data
	// ones. (`.data` itself arrived at P1.0d.5b — see `dataPrelude`.) The IAT relocations remain
	// for the PE writer to patch. P1.2's strings will register into this same table and need no
	// new pipeline.
	var result = CodeResult.create(code, mainCallFixupOffset: entryOffset, relocations: relocations, globalData: globalData, targetModule: targetModule, stdModule: stdModule, rdata: globalData.rdataImage(), rdataRelocations: rdataRelocations, symdata: globalData.symdataImage, covDataSectionBytes: ByteArray.create())
	result.debugSymbols = debugSymbols
	result.dataPrelude = globalData.dataSectionImage()
	result.jumpTableRelocs = jumpTableRelocs
	// The chunks' own PRE-LINK bytes, for the one reader `code` cannot serve: a ```RequiredRuntime
	// golden over a hand-assembled chunk (`mrt_panic`, `__gt_context_switch`, …), which has no
	// `IrFunction` and therefore no IR body to print. See `CodeResult.codeChunks`.
	result.codeChunks = chunks
	return result
end 'concatX64FunctionChunks'

// The offset of a code→data reference's target WITHIN ITS OWN SECTION. The kind is what says
// which section that is — the same discriminator the PE writer patches by — so this is the one
// place the backend maps a relocation kind to the table that can answer for it.
//
// `.data` and `.rdata` are two tables with two offset indexes inside one `GlobalDataTable`, and
// asking the wrong one is not a near miss: both would answer a small integer, and the reference
// would resolve into the wrong section's bytes. So a kind with no table here PANICS rather than
// defaulting to either.
function sectionOffsetOfReloc(globalData GlobalDataTable, reloc RdataRelocation) returns CodeOffset
	return match reloc.kind 'kind'
		ripRelDisp32 gives globalData.rdataOffsetOf(reloc.rdataLabel)
		dataSectionRipRelDisp32 gives globalData.dataSectionOffsetOf(reloc.rdataLabel)
		symdataRipRelDisp32 panic("sectionOffsetOfReloc: '{reloc.rdataLabel}' is a `.symtab` reference, and shv2's backend emits none — symdata grows back at its own milestone")
		covdataRipRelDisp32 panic("sectionOffsetOfReloc: '{reloc.rdataLabel}' is a `.covdata` reference, and shv2's backend emits none — coverage grows back at its own milestone")
		arm64Adr to covdataArm64PageOff panic("sectionOffsetOfReloc: '{reloc.rdataLabel}' carries an arm64 relocation kind in the x64 backend")
	end 'kind'
end 'sectionOffsetOfReloc'

// Patch every `call rel32` to the rel32 displacement of its named target, via the
// shared patchChunkRel32 (same "measured from end of field" convention as the
// intra-function block jumps).
function resolveCallFixups(code ByteArray, labelOffsets FuncLabelOffsetMap, callFixups CallFixupArray)
	for fixup in callFixups 'resolve'
		let targetOffset = try labelOffsets.get(fixup.targetName) otherwise panic("resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '{String.from(fixup.targetName)}'")
		patchChunkRel32(code, codeOffset: fixup.codeOffset, targetOffset: targetOffset, what: PatchSite.x64CallRel32)
	end 'resolve'
end 'resolveCallFixups'
